 false}, {"ayah": 117, "text": "مَا قُلْتُ لَهُمْ إِلَّا مَآ أَمَرْتَنِى بِهِۦٓ أَنِ ٱعْبُدُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ رَبِّى وَرَبَّكُمْۚ وَكُنتُ عَلَيْهِمْ شَهِيدًا مَّا دُمْتُ فِيهِمْۖ فَلَمَّا تَوَفَّيْتَنِى كُنتَ أَنتَ ٱلرَّقِيبَ عَلَيْهِمْۚ وَأَنتَ عَلَىٰ كُلِّ شَىْءٍ شَهِيدٌ ", "translation": "I said not to them except what You commanded me - to worship Allāh, my Lord and your Lord. And I was a witness over them as long as I was among them; but when You took me up, You were the Observer over them, and You are, over all things, Witness.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "قُلْتُ", "translit": "ُtْlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مَآ", "translit": "ٓāَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَمَرْتَنِى", "translit": "àِnَtْrَmَaʾ", "root": "امر"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "بِهِۦٓ", "translit": "ٓۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أَنِ", "translit": "ِnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱعْبُدُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُdُbْʿعٱ", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "رَبِّى", "translit": "àِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَرَبَّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَّbَrَw", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَكُنتُ", "translit": "ُtnُkَw", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "شَهِيدًا", "translit": "āًdyِhَhs", "root": "شهد"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "مَّا", "translit": "āَّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "دُمْتُ", "translit": "ُtْmُd", "root": "دوم"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "فِيهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "فَلَمَّا", "translit": "āَّmَlَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "تَوَفَّيْتَنِى", "translit": "àِnَtْyَّfَwَt", "root": "وفي"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "كُنتَ", "translit": "َtnُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "أَنتَ", "translit": "َtnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "ٱلرَّقِيبَ", "translit": "َbyِqَّrlٱ", "root": "رقب"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "وَأَنتَ", "translit": "َtnَaʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "كُلِّ", "translit": "ِّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "شَىْءٍ", "translit": "ٍءْàَhs", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 29, "arabic": "شَهِيدٌ", "translit": "ٌdyِhَhs", "root": "شهد"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 118, "text": "إِن تُعَذِّبْهُمْ فَإِنَّهُمْ عِبَادُكَۖ وَإِن تَغْفِرْ لَهُمْ فَإِنَّكَ أَنتَ ٱلْعَزِيزُ ٱلْحَكِيمُ ", "translation": "If You should punish them - indeed they are Your servants; but if You forgive them - indeed it is You who is the Exalted in Might, the Wise.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تُعَذِّبْهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhْbِّhdَʿعُt", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فَإِنَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّnِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "عِبَادُكَ", "translit": "َkُdāَbِʿع", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَإِن", "translit": "nِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "تَغْفِرْ", "translit": "ْrِfْغَt", "root": "غفر"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فَإِنَّكَ", "translit": "َkَّnِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "أَنتَ", "translit": "َtnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلْعَزِيزُ", "translit": "ُzyِzَʿعْlٱ", "root": "عزز"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْحَكِيمُ", "translit": "ُmyِkَḥْlٱ", "root": "حكم"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 119, "text": "قَالَ ٱللَّهُ هَٰذَا يَوْمُ يَنفَعُ ٱلصَّٰدِقِينَ صِدْقُهُمْۚ لَهُمْ جَنَّٰتٌ تَجْرِى مِن تَحْتِهَا ٱلْأَنْهَٰرُ خَٰلِدِينَ فِيهَآ أَبَدًاۚ رَّضِىَ ٱللَّهُ عَنْهُمْ وَرَضُوا۟ عَنْهُۚ ذَٰلِكَ ٱلْفَوْزُ ٱلْعَظِيمُ ", "translation": "Allāh will say, \"This is the Day when the truthful will benefit from their truthfulness.\" For them are gardens [in Paradise] beneath which rivers flow, wherein they will abide forever, Allāh being pleased with them, and they with Him. That is the great attainment.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "هَٰذَا", "translit": "āَhdَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَوْمُ", "translit": "ُmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يَنفَعُ", "translit": "ُʿعَfnَy", "root": "نفع"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلصَّٰدِقِينَ", "translit": "َnyِqِdَّٰṣlٱ", "root": "صدق"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "صِدْقُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُqْdِṣ", "root": "صدق"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "جَنَّٰتٌ", "translit": "ٌtَّٰnَjg", "root": "جنن"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "تَجْرِى", "translit": "àِrْjgَt", "root": "جري"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "تَحْتِهَا", "translit": "āَhِtْḥَt", "root": "تحت"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلْأَنْهَٰرُ", "translit": "ُrَٰhْnَaʾْlٱ", "root": "نهر"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "خَٰلِدِينَ", "translit": "َnyِdِlَٰhk", "root": "خلد"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "فِيهَآ", "translit": "ٓāَhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "أَبَدًا", "translit": "āًdَbَaʾ", "root": "ابد"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "رَّضِىَ", "translit": "َàِḍَّr", "root": "رضو"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "عَنْهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhْnَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "وَرَضُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُḍَrَw", "root": "رضو"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "عَنْهُ", "translit": "ُhْnَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "ٱلْفَوْزُ", "translit": "ُzْwَfْlٱ", "root": "فوز"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "ٱلْعَظِيمُ", "translit": "ُmyِẓَʿعْlٱ", "root": "عظم"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 120, "text": "لِلَّهِ مُلْكُ ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَٱلْأَرْضِ وَمَا فِيهِنَّۚ وَهُوَ عَلَىٰ كُلِّ شَىْءٍ قَدِيرٌۢ ", "translation": "To Allāh belongs the dominion of the heavens and the earth and whatever is within them. And He is over all things competent.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لِلَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّlِl", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مُلْكُ", "translit": "ُkْlُm", "root": "ملك"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰwَٰmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱَw", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فِيهِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَهُوَ", "translit": "َwُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "كُلِّ", "translit": "ِّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "شَىْءٍ", "translit": "ٍءْàَhs", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "قَدِيرٌۢ", "translit": "ٌۢryِdَq", "root": "قدر"}], "hasIhya": true}], "tafsir": {"1": [{"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذلك ذم الله تعالى قوما جعلوا لله ما يكرهون فقال تعالى ويجعلون لله ما يكر", "english": "The heart is not treated save with the compound electuary (maʿjūn) of knowledge and works. And this condition among the conditions of almsgiving runs in the same course as humility (al-khushūʿ) runs in the prayer. This is established by his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"A man has nothing of his prayer save that of which he was conscious.\" And this is like his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"Allah does not accept the charity of the one who bestows it with reproach (al-mannān).\" And like His saying — Exalted is He: {«لا تبطلوا صدقاتكم بالمن والأذى»} (\"Do not nullify your charities by reproach and injury\"). As for the legal opinion (fatwā) of the jurist that the alms fall in their proper place and that one's liability is discharged without this condition — that is another discussion, and we have already pointed to its meaning in the Book of Prayer.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Anger and Hatred", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Condemnation of Anger and Hatred'", "badge": "Insight"}], "2": [{"arabic": "والزكاة في نعت الصالحين والمؤمنين وقال تعالى وتعاونوا على البر والتقوى ولا تعاونوا على الإثم والعدوان وهو أمر جزم ومعنى التعاون الحث عليه وتسهيل طرق الخير وسد سبل الشر والعدوان بحسب الإمكان وقال تعالى لولا ينهاهم الربانيون والأحبار عن قولهم الإثم وأكلهم السحت لبئس ما كانوا يصنعون فبين أنهم أثموا بترك النهي وقال تعالى فلولا كان من القرون من قبلكم أولوا بقية ينهون عن الفساد في الأرض الآية فبين أنه أهلك جميعهم إلا قليلا منهم كانوا ينهون عن الفساد وقال تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا كونوا قوامين بالقسط شهداء لله ولو على أنفسكم أو الوالدين والأقربين وذلك هو الأ", "english": "Know that the verses indicating the obligation of commanding right and forbidding wrong are numerous. Among them is His saying, may He be exalted: {«Let there arise from among you a nation inviting to good, commanding right and forbidding wrong — and those are the successful»} (Āl ʿImrān 3:104). In this verse is the declaration of obligation, for His saying, may He be exalted, \"Let there be\" (wal-takun) is a command, and the apparent meaning of a command is obligation. In it also is the declaration that success (falāḥ) is contingent upon it, for He restricted it and said: {«And those are the successful»}. In it also is the declaration that it is a collective obligation (farḍ kifāyah), not an individual obligation (farḍ ʿayn), and that when a group undertakes it, the obligation falls from the rest — for He did not say, \"Let all of you be commanders of right,\" but rather said: {«Let there arise from among you a nation»}. Thus, whenever one person or a group undertakes it, the burden falls from the others, and success is particularized to those who undertake it and directly perform it. But if all of creation were to hold back from it, the burden would encompass all those capable of it without exception.", "book_title": "Enjoining Good and Forbidding Evil", "section_title_ar": "Section 521", "section_title_en": "Section 521", "section_index": 521, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Enjoining Good and Forbidding Evil'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذلك ذم الله تعالى قوما جعلوا لله ما يكرهون فقال تعال", "english": "The heart is not treated save with the compound electuary (maʿjūn) of knowledge and works. And this condition among the conditions of almsgiving runs in the same course as humility (al-khushūʿ) runs in the prayer. This is established by his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"A man has nothing of his prayer save that of which he was conscious.\" And this is like his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"Allah does not accept the charity of the one who bestows it with reproach (al-mannān).\" And like His saying — Exalted is He: {«لا تبطلوا صدقاتكم بالمن والأذى»} (\"Do not nullify your charities by reproach and injury\"). As for the legal opinion (fatwā) of the jurist that the alms fall in their proper place and that one's liability is discharged without this condition — that is another discussion, and we have already pointed to its meaning in the Book of Prayer.", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Insight"}], "3": [{"arabic": "وتمام الإسلام وكمال الدين فيه أنزل الله عز وجل {اليوم أكملت لكم دينكم وأتممت عليكم نعمتي ورضيت لكم الإسلام دينا} وفيه قال صلى الله عليه وسلم من مات ولم يحج فليمت إن شاء يهوديا وإن شاء نصرانيا (1) فأعظم بعبادة يعدم الدين بفقدها الكمال ويساوي تاركها اليهود والنصارى في الضلال وأجدر بها أن تصرف العناية إلى شرحها وتفصيل أركانها وسننها وآدابها وفضائلها وأسرارها وجملة ذلك ينكشف بتوفيق الله عز وجل في ثلاثة أبواب الباب الأول في فضائلها وفضائل مكة والبيت العتيق وجمل أركان وشرائط وجوبها الباب الثاني في أعمالها الظاهرة على الترتيب من مبدأ السفر إلى الرجوع الباب الثال", "english": "To proceed: Know that the Pilgrimage (ḥajj), among the pillars of Islam and its foundational supports, is the worship of a lifetime, the seal of one's affairs, the completion of Islam, and the perfection of the religion. Concerning it, God — Mighty and Majestic — revealed: {«اليوم أكملت لكم دينكم وأتممت عليكم نعمتي ورضيت لكم الإسلام دينا»} (\"This day I have perfected for you your religion, and completed upon you My favor, and have chosen for you Islam as your religion\") {Q. 5:3}. And concerning it, the Prophet — peace and blessings be upon him — said: \"Whoever dies without having performed the Pilgrimage, let him die, if he wishes, as a Jew, and if he wishes, as a Christian.\" How great is a worship whose loss deprives the religion of its perfection, and whose abandoner is equated with Jews and Christians in error! And it is all the more fitting that careful attention be devoted to its exposition, the detailing of its pillars (arkān), its normative practices (sunan), its etiquettes (ādāb), its virtues (faḍā'il), and its inner secrets (asrār).", "book_title": "Mysteries of Hajj", "section_title_ar": "Section 191", "section_title_en": "The Third Chapter on Voluntary Fasting and the Arrangement of Daily Liturgies (Awrād) Therein", "section_index": 191, "anchors": "- Root: حمد (Ḥ-M-D)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of praise, commendation, and grateful acknowledgment of beneficence.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance — ḥamd is the conscious, deliberate act of praising God for His attributes and acts, distinct from mere shukr (thankfulness for benefits received).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Ḥaqīqah) — \"أحمد الله تعالى بجميع محامده\" (I praise God with all His praiseworthy attributes); Metaphorical (Majāz) — \"أحمدت صنيعه\" (I found his deed praiseworthy), extending to approval of conduct.\n- Root: وفي (W-F-Y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Completeness, fulfillment, reaching full measure.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance — wafā' signifies the perfect completion of a covenant or obligation; God's \"completion\" of His religion denotes its absolute finality and perfection.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — \"درهم واف\" (a full-weight dirham); Metaphorical — \"أوفى على المائة\" (he exceeded a hundred), indicating supererogatory completion beyond the obligatory.\n- Root: فصل (F-Ṣ-L)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Separation, distinction, decisive division between two things.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance — faṣl is the act of distinguishing truth from falsehood, as in \"Yawm al-Faṣl\" (the Day of Decision); here, it denotes the structural division of a book into chapters.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — \"فصلت الشاة\" (I severed the joints of the sheep); Metaphorical — \"هذا الأمر فيصل\" (this matter is decisive, cutting through disputes).\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The structural division of discourse into clearly demarcated sections (fuṣūl) mirrors the syntactic principle of separating elements for clarity, ensuring each unit stands complete and unambiguous.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Mysteries of Hajj'", "badge": "Insight"}], "6": [{"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذلك ذم الله تعالى قوما جعلوا لله ما يكرهون فقال تعال", "english": "The heart is not treated save with the compound electuary (maʿjūn) of knowledge and works. And this condition among the conditions of almsgiving runs in the same course as humility (al-khushūʿ) runs in the prayer. This is established by his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"A man has nothing of his prayer save that of which he was conscious.\" And this is like his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"Allah does not accept the charity of the one who bestows it with reproach (al-mannān).\" And like His saying — Exalted is He: {«لا تبطلوا صدقاتكم بالمن والأذى»} (\"Do not nullify your charities by reproach and injury\"). As for the legal opinion (fatwā) of the jurist that the alms fall in their proper place and that one's liability is discharged without this condition — that is another discussion, and we have already pointed to its meaning in the Book of Prayer.", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Insight"}], "7": [{"arabic": "ولذلك قال عز وجل لعلهم يتذكرون وليتذكر أولوا الألباب واذكروا نعمة الله عليكم وميثاقه الذي واثقكم به ولقد يسرنا القرآن للذكر فهل من مدكر وتسمية هذا النمط تذكرا ليس ببعيد فكأن التذكر ضربان أحدهما أن يذكر صورة كانت حاضرة الوجود في قلبه لكن غابت بعد الوجود الآخر والآخر أن يذكر صورة كانت مضمنة فيه بالفطرة وهذه حقائق ظاهرة للناظر بنور البصيرة ثقيلة على من يستروجه (1) السماع والتقليد دون الكشف والعيان ولذلك تراه يتخبط في مثل هذه الآيات ويتعسف وفي تأويل التذكر بإقرار النفوس أنواعا من التعسفات ويتخايل إليه في الأخبار والآيات ضروب من المناقضات وربما يغل", "english": "I say: Know that the washing of the eye is forbidden — that is, the eye of insight (ʿayn al-baṣīrah) is not to be washed with the water of blind imitation and mere hearsay, for it is the organ by which the realities of the intellect are disclosed. And the divisions of what the name \"intellect\" (al-ʿaql) is applied to have become clear: it is said of the inborn faculty (al-gharīzah), and it is said of the knowledge that is acquired through that faculty, and it is said of the state of that knowledge when it governs the soul, and it is said of the very actions that proceed from that governance.", "book_title": "Book of Knowledge", "section_title_ar": "Section 68", "section_title_en": "The Washing of the Eye is Forbidden — On the True Meaning of Intellect (ʿAql) and Its Divisions", "section_index": 68, "anchors": "- Root: عقل (ʿAql)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes binding, restraining, and holding back — the intellect is that which restrains the soul from base desires and prevents it from straying.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العقل يقال للقوة المتهيئة لقبول العلم، ويقال للعلم الذي يستفيده الإنسان بتلك القوة عقل\" — al-ʿaql is said of the faculty prepared to receive knowledge, and it is also said of the knowledge acquired by that faculty. It is the instrument of divine discernment and the locus of taklīf (moral responsibility).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): the inborn faculty of discernment. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"نخلة لا تعقل الإبار إذا لم تقبله\" — a palm tree that does not accept pollination is said to be \"not to ʿaqil\" the pollen, extending the root to the sense of \"accepting/receiving.\" Thus, knowledge is called ʿaql metaphorically as its fruit.\n- Root: بصر (Baṣar)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Sight, perception — both of the eye and of the heart.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"البصر يقال للجارحة الناظرة... ويقال لقوة القلب المدركة: بصيرة وبصر\" — baṣar is said of the seeing organ, and of the perceiving power of the heart: baṣīrah and baṣar. The inner sight (baṣīrah) perceives immaterial realities.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: ocular vision. Metaphorical: \"هذه آية مبصرة\" — this is an illuminating sign; \"واجعلني بصيرة عليهم أي رقيبا وشاهدا\" — make me a watcher over them. The inner eye perceives what the outer eye cannot.\n- Root: فطر (Faṭara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To split, cleave, originate, create — the primordial act of divine creation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"أصل الفطر: الشق طولا... وفطر الله الخلق\" — the root is splitting lengthwise; God's \"fatr\" of creation is His originating of things. \"فطرة الله التي فطر الناس عليها\" — the primordial disposition upon which God originated mankind.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"فطر الله الخلق، وهو فاطر السموات: مبتدعها\" — God originated creation; He is the Originator of the heavens. \"وكل مولود يولد على الفطرة\" — every newborn is born upon the fiṭrah, i.e., the innate disposition receptive to the religion of truth.\n- Root: نفس (Nafs)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Self, soul, spirit; the innermost reality of a thing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النفس: الروح... وقوله: تعلم ما في نفسي ولا أعلم ما في نفسك\" — nafs is the spirit/soul; \"You know what is in my nafs and I know not what is in Your nafs\" — the nafs is the seat of inner assent and recognition.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the self. Metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه أي دمه\" — he poured out his nafs, i.e., his blood. The nafs is the locus of the primordial covenant (mīthāq).\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The construction \"إنما العاقل من آمن بالله\" restricts the predicate: the intellect is not merely a faculty of cleverness but is defined by its fruit — faith and God-consciousness (taqwā).", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Book of Knowledge'", "badge": "Insight"}], "8": [{"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذلك ذم الله تعالى قوما جعلوا لله ما يكرهون فقال تعال", "english": "The heart is not treated save with the compound electuary (maʿjūn) of knowledge and works. And this condition among the conditions of almsgiving runs in the same course as humility (al-khushūʿ) runs in the prayer. This is established by his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"A man has nothing of his prayer save that of which he was conscious.\" And this is like his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"Allah does not accept the charity of the one who bestows it with reproach (al-mannān).\" And like His saying — Exalted is He: {«لا تبطلوا صدقاتكم بالمن والأذى»} (\"Do not nullify your charities by reproach and injury\"). As for the legal opinion (fatwā) of the jurist that the alms fall in their proper place and that one's liability is discharged without this condition — that is another discussion, and we have already pointed to its meaning in the Book of Prayer.", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Insight"}], "11": [{"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذلك ذم الله تعالى قوما جعلوا لله ما يكرهون فقال تعال", "english": "The heart is not treated save with the compound electuary (maʿjūn) of knowledge and works. And this condition among the conditions of almsgiving runs in the same course as humility (al-khushūʿ) runs in the prayer. This is established by his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"A man has nothing of his prayer save that of which he was conscious.\" And this is like his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"Allah does not accept the charity of the one who bestows it with reproach (al-mannān).\" And like His saying — Exalted is He: {«لا تبطلوا صدقاتكم بالمن والأذى»} (\"Do not nullify your charities by reproach and injury\"). As for the legal opinion (fatwā) of the jurist that the alms fall in their proper place and that one's liability is discharged without this condition — that is another discussion, and we have already pointed to its meaning in the Book of Prayer.", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Insight"}], "13": [{"arabic": "وقوله {ولمن صبر وغفر إن ذلك لمن عزم الأمور} وقوله {فاعف عنهم واصفح إن الله يحب المحسنين} وقوله {وليعفوا وليصفحوا ألا تحبون أن يغفر الله لكم} وقوله {ادفع بالتي هي أحسن فإذا الذي بينك وبينه عداوة كأنه ولي حميم} وقوله {والكاظمين الغيظ والعافين عن الناس والله يحب المحسنين} وقوله {اجتنبوا كثيرا من الظن إن بعض الظن إثم ولا تجسسوا ولا يغتب بعضكم بعضا} ولما كسرت رباعيته وشج يوم أحد فجعل الدم يسيل على وجهه وهو يمسح الدم ويقول كيف يفلح قوم خضبوا وجه نبيهم بالدم وهو يدعوهم إلى ربهم (4) فأنزل الله تعالى {ليس لك من الأمر شيء} تأديبا له", "english": "The Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, was abundant in supplication and earnest entreaty, perpetually asking God Most High to adorn him with the beauties of refined conduct and the noble traits of character. Thus he would say in his supplication: \"O God, beautify my outward form and my inward character (khuluqī wa-khalqī)\" (1), and he would say: \"O God, protect me from the reprehensible traits of character\" (2). God Most High answered his supplication, fulfilling His word, Exalted is He: {«ادعوني أستجب لكم»} [\"Call upon Me, I will answer you\"] (Ghafir 40:60). So He sent down upon him the Qur'an and disciplined him through it, and his character became the Qur'an.", "book_title": "Etiquette of Living and Prophetic Character", "section_title_ar": "Section 559", "section_title_en": "The Prophet's Discipline Through the Qur'an and the Invitation to Noble Moral Traits", "section_index": 559, "anchors": "- Root: خلق (khalaqa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of measuring, fashioning, and creating upon a precise estimation; also used for innate disposition and character.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الخلق أصله: التقدير المستقيم\" — the root denotes straight/right estimation; used for bringing something into being from nothing (ibdāʿ) and for the moral constitution of the soul.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"خلق الخراز الأديم\" — the leather-worker measures before cutting. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"خلق الله الخلق: أوجده على تقدير أوجبته الحكمة\" — God's creation is bringing into existence upon a measure required by wisdom; and \"له خلق حسن\" — he has beautiful character.\n- Root: كرم (karuma)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Generosity, nobility, honor; the root of karīm (noble/generous) and makārim (noble traits).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الكرم إذا وصف الله تعالى به فهو اسم لإحسانه وإنعامه المتظاهر\" — when attributed to God, it denotes His manifest beneficence; when attributed to man, it denotes praiseworthy morals and deeds that appear from him.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"كرم علينا فلان كرامة\" — he showed generosity. Metaphorical: \"قوم كرم\" — a noble people; \"كرم السحاب تكريما: جاد بمطره\" — the cloud was generous with its rain.\n- Root: أدب (adaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Refinement, discipline, cultivation of character; calling to a gathering (ma'dabah) — the root implies assembling and summoning toward excellence.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Discipline (ta'dīb) is the cultivation of the soul through praiseworthy traits and the restraint from blameworthy ones.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"أدبته تأديبا\" — I disciplined him. Metaphorical: \"أدبه الله\" — God disciplined him, i.e., refined his character through divine instruction.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — \"إنما أدبه القرآن\" restricts the means of the Prophet's discipline exclusively to the Qur'an, affirming that the Qur'an alone was the sufficient and complete instrument of his moral formation.", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'Etiquette of Living and Prophetic Character'", "badge": "Du'a"}], "16": [{"arabic": "نورا يمشي به في الناس وحيث ذكر النور والظلمة أراد به العلم والجهل كقوله {يخرجهم من الظلمات إلى النور} وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم يا أيها الناس اعقلوا عن ربكم وتواصوا بالعقل تعرفوا ما أمرتم به وما نهيتم عنه واعلموا أنه ينجدكم عند ربكم واعلموا أن العاقل من أطاع الله وإن كان دميم المنظر حقير الخطر دنيء المنزلة رث الهيئة وأن الجاهل من عصى الله تعالى وإن كان جميل المنظر عظيم الخطر شريف المنزلة حسن الهيئة فصيحا نطوقا فالقردة والخنازير أعقل عند الله تعالى ممن عصاه ولا تغتر بتعظيم أهل الدنيا إياهم فإنهم من الخاسرين (2) وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم أول ما خلق", "english": "The Book of Seclusion ('Uzlah) — its explanation, God Most High willing. For this reason, Yūsuf ibn Asbāṭ wrote to Ḥudhayfah al-Marʿashī: \"What is your opinion of one who remains, finding no one with whom to remember God Most High except that he is either sinful, or his remembrance in company is itself an act of disobedience — and that because he finds no one of his own kind?\" And indeed he spoke truly, for mixing with people is never free from backbiting (ghībah), or listening to backbiting, or remaining silent upon wrongdoing (munkar). And the best of his states is that he either imparts knowledge or acquires it. Yet were this poor wretch to reflect, and to know that his imparting of knowledge is never free from the taints of ostentation (riyā'), the seeking of followers, and the desire for leadership (ri'āsah), he would know that the one who acquires knowledge from him only seeks to make it an instrument for pursuing the world and a means toward evil — so that the teacher becomes his helper therein, his support and his backer, preparing its causes for him, like one who sells a sword to a highway robber. For knowledge is like the sword: its soundness for good is like the soundness of the sword for warfare. And for this reason, it is not permitted to sell it to one whom one knows, by the indications of his circumstances, intends to use it to aid in cutting off the road.", "book_title": "Book of Knowledge", "section_title_ar": "Section 65", "section_title_en": "The Book of Seclusion ('Uzlah) — Section 65, and The Seventh Book: On the Intellect, Its Nobility, Its Reality, and Its Divisions", "section_index": 65, "anchors": "- Root: عقل (ʿaql)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes binding, restraining, and holding back; the intellect is that which restrains the soul from base desires and prevents it from straying.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العقل يقال للقوة المتهيئة لقبول العلم، ويقال للعلم الذي يستفيده الإنسان بتلك القوة عقل\" — The intellect is said of the faculty prepared for the reception of knowledge, and it is also said of the knowledge which man acquires by means of that faculty.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): intellectual apprehension and understanding. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"نخلة لا تعقل الإبار\" — a palm tree that does not accept pollination, i.e., does not grasp or take in.\n- Root: علم (ʿilm)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Knowledge as the mark or sign by which a thing is known; the trace or landmark that guides.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — Knowledge is the apprehension of a thing in its true reality; it is of two kinds: apprehension of the essence of a thing, and judgment upon a thing regarding what exists for it or is negated of it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: certain awareness. Metaphorical: \"هو من أعلام العلم الخافقه\" — he is among the lofty banners of knowledge; knowledge as a guiding landmark (maʿlam).\n- Root: نور (nūr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Light; the first to set up beacons (manār) on his path in his campaigns to guide his return.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النور: الضوء المنتشر الذي يعين على الإبصار\" — Light is the diffused radiance that aids vision; it is of two kinds: that perceived by the eye of inner insight (baṣīrah) — such as the light of the intellect and the light of the Qur'an — and that perceived by the eye of the head, such as the celestial bodies.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: illumination. Metaphorical: \"نور الأمر: بينه\" — he illuminated the matter: he made it clear; \"وهذا أنور من ذاك: أبين\" — this is more luminous than that: more evident.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and Focused Predication (al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd bi-innamā) — governing the exclusive attribution of a predicate to a subject, as in the author's restriction of the scholar's condition to one of two men, and the exclusion of the third.", "content_type": "dhikr", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on constant remembrance of God and contemplative invocations, from 'Book of Knowledge'", "badge": "Dhikr"}, {"arabic": "{يخرجهم من الظلمات إلى النور} وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم يا أيها الناس اعقلوا عن ربكم وتواصوا بالعقل تعرفوا ما أمرتم به وما نهيتم عنه واعلموا أنه ينجدكم عند ربكم واعلموا أن العاقل من أطاع الله وإن كان دميم المنظر حقير الخطر دنيء المنزلة رث الهيئة وأن الجاهل من عصى الله تعالى وإن كان جميل المنظر عظيم الخطر شريف المنزلة حسن الهيئة فصيحا نطوقا فالقردة والخنازير أعقل عند الله تعالى ممن عصاه ولا تغتر بتعظيم أهل الدنيا إياهم فإنهم من الخاسرين (2) وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم أول ما خل", "english": "The Book of Seclusion ('Uzlah) — its explanation, God Most High willing. For this reason, Yūsuf ibn Asbāṭ wrote to Ḥudhayfah al-Marʿashī: \"What is your opinion of one who remains, finding no one with whom to remember God Most High except that he is either sinful, or his remembrance in company is itself an act of disobedience — and that because he finds no one of his own kind?\" And indeed he spoke truly, for mixing with people is never free from backbiting (ghībah), or listening to backbiting, or remaining silent upon wrongdoing (munkar). And the best of his states is that he either imparts knowledge or acquires it. Yet were this poor wretch to reflect, and to know that his imparting of knowledge is never free from the taints of ostentation (riyā'), the seeking of followers, and the desire for leadership (ri'āsah), he would know that the one who acquires knowledge from him only seeks to make it an instrument for pursuing the world and a means toward evil — so that the teacher becomes his helper therein, his support and his backer, preparing its causes for him, like one who sells a sword to a highway robber. For knowledge is like the sword: its soundness for good is like the soundness of the sword for warfare. And for this reason, it is not permitted to sell it to one whom one knows, by the indications of his circumstances, intends to use it to aid in cutting off the road.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Delusion", "section_title_ar": "Section 65", "section_title_en": "The Book of Seclusion ('Uzlah) — Section 65, and The Seventh Book: On the Intellect, Its Nobility, Its Reality, and Its Divisions", "section_index": 65, "anchors": "- Root: عقل (ʿaql)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes binding, restraining, and holding back; the intellect is that which restrains the soul from base desires and prevents it from straying.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العقل يقال للقوة المتهيئة لقبول العلم، ويقال للعلم الذي يستفيده الإنسان بتلك القوة عقل\" — The intellect is said of the faculty prepared for the reception of knowledge, and it is also said of the knowledge which man acquires by means of that faculty.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): intellectual apprehension and understanding. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"نخلة لا تعقل الإبار\" — a palm tree that does not accept pollination, i.e., does not grasp or take in.\n- Root: علم (ʿilm)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Knowledge as the mark or sign by which a thing is known; the trace or landmark that guides.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — Knowledge is the apprehension of a thing in its true reality; it is of two kinds: apprehension of the essence of a thing, and judgment upon a thing regarding what exists for it or is negated of it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: certain awareness. Metaphorical: \"هو من أعلام العلم الخافقه\" — he is among the lofty banners of knowledge; knowledge as a guiding landmark (maʿlam).\n- Root: نور (nūr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Light; the first to set up beacons (manār) on his path in his campaigns to guide his return.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النور: الضوء المنتشر الذي يعين على الإبصار\" — Light is the diffused radiance that aids vision; it is of two kinds: that perceived by the eye of inner insight (baṣīrah) — such as the light of the intellect and the light of the Qur'an — and that perceived by the eye of the head, such as the celestial bodies.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: illumination. Metaphorical: \"نور الأمر: بينه\" — he illuminated the matter: he made it clear; \"وهذا أنور من ذاك: أبين\" — this is more luminous than that: more evident.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and Focused Predication (al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd bi-innamā) — governing the exclusive attribution of a predicate to a subject, as in the author's restriction of the scholar's condition to one of two men, and the exclusion of the third.", "content_type": "dhikr", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on constant remembrance of God and contemplative invocations, from 'Condemnation of Delusion'", "badge": "Dhikr"}], "18": [{"arabic": "حبيب الله فقال {قل فلم يعذبكم بذنوبكم} وقد روى أنس عن النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم أنه قال إذا أحب الله تعالى عبدا لم يضره ذنب والتائب من الذنب كمن لا ذنب له ثم تلا إن الله يحب التوابين (1) ومعناه أنه إذا أحبه تاب عليه قبل الموت فلم تضره الذنوب الماضية وإن كثرت كما لا يضر الكفر الماضي بعد الإسلام وقد اشترط الله تعالى للمحبة غفران الذنب فقال {قل إن كنتم تحبون الله فاتبعوني يحببكم الله ويغفر لكم ذنوبكم} وقال رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم إن الله تعالى يعطي الدنيا من يحب ومن لا يحب و", "english": "Know that the evidences of the Qur'an are manifest and numerous that God—exalted is He—loves His servant; thus, it is imperative to understand the meaning of this [love]. Let us first present the evidences of His love. God—exalted is He—said: {«يُحِبُّهُمْ وَيُحِبُّونَهُ»} [He loves them, and they love Him] (Q 5:54). And He said: {«إِنَّ اللَّهَ يُحِبُّ الَّذِينَ يُقَاتِلُونَ فِي سَبِيلِهِ صَفًّا»} [Indeed, God loves those who fight in His cause in a row] (Q 61:4). And He said: {«إِنَّ اللَّهَ يُحِبُّ التَّوَّابِينَ وَيُحِبُّ الْمُتَطَهِّرِينَ»} [Indeed, God loves those who repent and loves those who purify themselves] (Q 2:222). For this reason, He—glorified is He—rebuked the one who claimed to be God's beloved, saying: {«قُلْ فَلِمَ يُعَذِّبُكُمْ بِذُنُوبِكُمْ»} [Say: Then why does He punish you for your sins?] (Q 5:18). It is narrated that Anas reported the Prophet—peace be upon him—as saying: \"When God—exalted is He—loves a servant, no sin harms him, and the one who repents from sin is like one who has no sin.\" Then he recited: {«إِنَّ اللَّهَ يُحِبُّ التَّوَّابِينَ»} [Indeed, God loves those who repent] (Q 2:222). Its meaning is that when He loves him, He grants him repentance before death, so past sins do not harm him, however numerous they may be—just as past disbelief does not harm one after embracing Islam. God—exalted is He—has made the forgiveness of sin a condition for [this] love, saying: {«قُلْ إِنْ كُنْتُمْ تُحِبُّونَ اللَّهَ فَاتَّبِعُونِي يُحْبِبْكُمُ اللَّهُ وَيَغْفِرْ لَكُمْ ذُنُوبَكُمْ»} [Say: If you love God, then follow me; God will love you and forgive you your sins] (Q 3:31). The Messenger of God—peace be upon him—said: \"God—exalted is He—gives the world to those He loves and those He does not love, but He does not give faith except to those He loves.\" And the Messenger of God—peace be upon him—said: \"Whoever humbles himself before God, God raises him; whoever is arrogant, God lowers him; and whoever remembers God much, God loves him.\" And he—peace be upon him—said, relaying from God—exalted is He: \"The servant continues to draw near to Me with supererogatory acts until I love him; and when I love him, I become his hearing with which he hears, and his sight with which he sees\" (ḥadīth). Zayd ibn Aslam said: \"God indeed loves a servant until, from the intensity of His love for him, He says: 'Do what you wish, for I have forgiven you.'\" The expressions of love [in scripture] are beyond enumeration.", "book_title": "Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment", "section_title_ar": "Section 1173", "section_title_en": "On the Reality of God's Love for His Servant and Its Interpretation", "section_index": 1173, "anchors": "- Root: ح ب ب (ḥ-b-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to love, to cherish; the root denotes inclination and attachment of the heart.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological usage, love (maḥabbah) toward God is a real inclination of the soul toward what is agreeable; toward creation, it is metaphorical, signifying divine will, favor, and the unveiling of veils.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) for creatures—an emotional inclination; metaphorical (majāz) for God—expressing volitional acts like drawing near, forgiving, and honoring.\n- Root: ق ر ب (q-r-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Proximity, nearness; both spatial and relational.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Qurb denotes nearness in station, rank, and favor, not physical location; God's \"nearness\" to a servant is by unveiling and honor.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: spatial closeness; metaphorical: closeness in rank, virtue, or divine acceptance (e.g., \"qaruba min Allāh\" = drew near to God by obedience).\n- Root: و ل ي (w-l-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be near, to govern, to befriend; walāyah implies closeness and guardianship.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Wilāyah of God for the believer is His care and guidance; the servant's wilāyah for God is loyal adherence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: adjacency; metaphorical: protective friendship and love.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and specification (bāb al-ḥaṣr wa-l-taqyīd bi-innamā)—used to delimit meanings when terms are applied to God versus creatures, ensuring no equivocation.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment'", "badge": "Insight"}], "19": [{"arabic": "وسلم على فترة من الرسل وكان شرع عيسى عليه السلام قد مضى عليه قريب من ستمائة سنة والناس منقسمون إلى مكذبين له من اليهود وعبدة الأوثان وإلى مصدقين له قد شاع الفسق فيهم كما شاع في زماننا الآن والكفار مخاطبون بفروع الشريعة والأموال كانت في أيدي المكذبين له والمصدقين أما المكذبون فكانوا يتعاملون بغير شرع عيسى عليه السلام وأما المصدقون فكان يتساهلون مع أصل التصديق", "english": "As for the third [possibility]—namely, restricting oneself to the measure of necessity without any increase upon it, while making no distinction between one property and another, whether it be taken by usurpation (ghaṣb), theft (sariqah), mutual consent (tarāḍī), or however it may happen—this is the removal of the Law's barrier between the corrupters and the kinds of corruption. Hands would then stretch forth in usurpation, theft, and varieties of injustice, and there would be no way to deter them from it, for they would say: \"The possessor of a hand is not distinguished from us by any entitlement, for it is forbidden to him and to us alike. The possessor has only the measure of his necessity. If he is in need, then we too are in need; and if what you have taken in my right exceeds your necessity, then you have stolen it from one who exceeds his need for his day.\" And if the need of the day and the year is not to be regarded, then what are we to regard? And how is this to be regulated? This leads to the nullification of the Law's governance and the incitement of the people of corruption to corruption. Thus nothing remains but the fourth possibility—namely, that it be said: Every possessor of a hand has a right to what is in his hand, and he has priority over it; it is not permissible that it be taken from him by theft or usurpation, but rather it is taken with his consent. And mutual consent (tarāḍī) is the path of the Law. And since it is not permissible except by mutual consent, mutual consent itself has a detailed methodology (minhāj) in the Law, upon which the [worldly] interests depend. For if this detail is not taken into account, then the very foundation of mutual consent would not be established, and its elaboration would be rendered inoperative.", "book_title": "The Lawful and Prohibited", "section_title_ar": "Section 354", "section_title_en": "On the Limits of Acquisition and the Necessity of Legal Proprietary Norms", "section_index": 354, "anchors": "- Root: قدر (qadr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core measure, extent, limit, or decree; that by which a thing is measured or determined.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): When applied to God, absolute power (qudrah) negates incapacity; when applied to man, it denotes a capacity restricted by need and circumstance—never absolute, always qualified.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqiqah): \"measure, amount, destiny.\" Metaphorical (majaz): \"a far-reaching steed\" (faras baʿīd al-qadr)—extension beyond the ordinary, applicable here to the overreach of tyrants' hands.\n- Root: حاج (ḥāj)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Need, want, necessity—entailing both lack and longing for fulfillment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-ḥājah ilā al-shay': al-faqr ilayhi maʿa maḥabbati-hi\"—poverty toward a thing coupled with desire for it; collective: ḥājāt, ḥawāʾij.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: genuine exigency. Metaphorical: not applicable here—remains strictly literal in juristic discourse.\n- Root: شرع (sharaʿa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A clear, well-trodden path; the source from which one drinks.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-sharʿ: nahj al-ṭarīq al-wāḍiḥ\"—the manifest way; then transferred (istiʿārah) to the divine law (sharīʿah) as the path of God's command.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the revealed law and its ordinances. Metaphorical: \"the camel extends its neck\" (sharraʿa al-baʿīr sharāʿahu)—likened to a ship's sail; here, the law's detailed provisions (tafṣīl) are the operative extension of its foundational principle (aṣl).\n- Root: ايد (ayd)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Strength, power, support; to fortify.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Ayyadtu-hu bi-rūḥ al-qudus\"—I strengthened him; God's ʾayd is His mighty power. \"Wa-al-samāʾa banaynāhā bi-ayd\"—by [Our] strength.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical or metaphysical might. Metaphorical: \"abundant hospitality\" (ādat ḍiyāfatuhu)—an overflowing provision; here, the \"hand\" (yad) of possession as the locus of legal entitlement.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition)—The permissibility of interposing elements between a governing word and its governed prepositional phrase depends on whether the intervening clause can stand independently; likewise, the law's foundational principle (aṣl al-tarāḍī) cannot be severed from its detailed application (tafṣīl) without rendering the whole inoperative.", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'The Lawful and Prohibited'", "badge": "Ethics"}], "23": [{"arabic": "أما من الآيات فقد قال تعالى وعلى الله فتوكلوا إن كنتم مؤمنين وقال عز وجل وعلى الله فليتوكل المتوكلون وقال تعالى ومن يتوكل على الله فهو حسبه وقال سبحانه وتعالى إن الله يحب المتوكلين وأعظم بمقام موسوم بمحبة الله تعالى صاحبه ومضمون كفاية الله تعالى ملابسه فمن الله تعالى حسبه وكافيه ومحبه ومراعيه فقد فاز الفوز العظيم فإن المحبوب لا يعذب ولا يبعد ولا يحجب وقال تعالى أليس الله بكاف عبده فطالب الكفاية من غيره والتارك للتوكل هو المكذب لهذه الآية فإنه سؤال في معرض استنطاق بالحق كقوله تعالى هل أتى على الإنسان", "english": "Praise be to God, the Disposer of the dominion (mulk) and the spiritual sovereignty (malakūt), the One alone in majesty (ʿizzah) and omnipotence (jabarūt), Who raised the heaven without any pillar, and apportioned therein the provisions of His servants; Who turned the eyes of those possessed of hearts and intellects away from the observation of intermediaries and causes (al-wasāʾiṭ wa-l-asbāb) toward the Causer of causes (musabbib al-asbāb); and Who lifted their concern from turning toward anything other than Him, and from relying upon any disposer besides Him. Thus they worshipped none but Him alone — in knowledge that He is the One, the Unique, the Eternal Refuge (al-ṣamad), the God — and in verification that all classes of creatures are servants like themselves, from whom no provision is sought, and that there is no atom but God created it, and no beast but upon God is its sustenance. When they verified that He is the Guarantor of His servants' provision and the Surety for it, they relied upon Him and said: {«حسبنا الله ونعم الوكيل»} — \"God is sufficient for us, and what an excellent Guardian!\" (Q. 3:173).", "book_title": "Monotheism and Reliance", "section_title_ar": "Section 1102", "section_title_en": "The Book of Divine Unity (Tawḥīd) and Reliance (Tawakkul) — Book Five of the Quarter of Deliverance from The Revival of the Religious Sciences", "section_index": 1102, "anchors": "- Root: وكل (w-k-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of delegating, entrusting, and appointing a deputy (wakīl) to manage one's affairs.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"التوكيل: أن تعتمد على غيرك وتجعله نائبا عنك\" — Reliance (tawakkul) is the act of depending upon another and making him a deputy over your affair; its theological force is that God alone is the sufficient Guardian (wakīl), as in {«وكفى بالله وكيلا»}.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): appointing an agent; Metaphorical (majāz): the weak man who leans on others (وكل ووكلة تكلة). In the divine context, tawakkul is a real, not figurative, transfer of one's trust to God as the sole efficient cause.\n- Root: سبب (s-b-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A rope, cord, or means by which one ascends or reaches something; extended to any intermediary cause.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"سمي كل ما يتوصل به إلى شيء سببا\" — Every means by which one reaches a goal is termed a cause (sabab); God is the \"Musabbib al-Asbāb\" (Causer of causes), transcending the causal ropes He suspends.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a physical cord; Metaphorical: any linkage or connection. Here, the \"gaze upon causes\" (mulāḥaẓat al-asbāb) is a metaphorical veiling of the true Agent.\n- Root: حسب (ḥ-s-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To count, reckon, suffice; derived: ḥasb (sufficiency), ḥasīb (one who suffices, or the Reckoner).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحساب: استعمال العدد\" — Counting; but in the divine attribute, \"ḥasb\" denotes God's all-sufficient guardianship, as in {«حسبنا الله ونعم الوكيل»}.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: reckoning by number; Metaphorical: \"احتسبت ما عند فلان: اختبرته وسبرته\" — to test and probe. God's being \"Ḥasb\" for the believer is a tested, verified sufficiency, not a mere claim.\n- Root: غمض (gh-m-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To close the eye, to be obscure, low-lying, or hidden ground.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"يستعار للتغافل والتساهل\" — closing the eye is metaphorically used for overlooking; here, the \"ghumūḍ\" (obscurity) of tawakkul is its conceptual hiddenness from the intellect.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a low, concealed place; Metaphorical: \"أمر غامض\" — a subtle, difficult affair. The text uses it for the doctrinal opacity of tawakkul.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Interposition of the prepositional phrase (باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور) — The periodic structure \"فلم يعبدوا إلا إياه\" and \"فقالوا حسبنا الله\" shows the separation of the operative verb from its object for rhetorical emphasis, permissible where the pause is complete and the meaning remains unbroken.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Monotheism and Reliance'", "badge": "Insight"}], "27": [{"arabic": "ثم قرأ {واتل عليهم نبأ ابني آدم بالحق} الآيات وإذا ذكر أصحاب رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم فأمسك وإذا ذكر القدر فاسكت وإذا ذكرت النجوم فاسكت وقال بكر بن عبد الله كان رجل يغشى بعض الملوك فيقوم بحذاء الملك فيقول أحسن إلى المحسن بإحسانه فإن المسيء سيكفيكه إساءته فحسده رجل على ذلك المقام والكلام فسعى به إلى الملك فقال إن هذا الذي يقوم بحذائك ويقول ما يقول زعم أن الملك أبخر فقال له الملك وكيف يصح ذلك عندي قال تدعوه إليك فإنه إذا دنا منك وضع يده على أنفه لئلا يشم ريح البخر فقال له انص", "english": "Know that envy (ḥasad) is likewise among the fruits of rancor (ḥiqd), and rancor is among the fruits of anger (ghaḍab) — so envy is a branch of a branch, while anger is the root of the root. Then envy has, among its blameworthy branches, that which can scarcely be enumerated. And there have come down, specifically in condemnation of envy, many reports (akhbār). The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: {«Envy consumes good deeds just as fire consumes wood»} (1). The ḥadīth: {«Do not cut one another off, nor turn your backs on one another, nor hate one another»} — agreed upon (muttafaq ʿalayh), and it has already preceded. (2) The ḥadīth of Anas: \"We were sitting one day with the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, when he said: 'There will now come upon you from this mountain pass a man from among the people of Paradise'\" — the ḥadīth in its entirety, and in it: that man said, \"I do not find in myself any rancor toward any of the Muslims, nor envy of anyone for the good that God has given him.\" Narrated by Aḥmad with a sound chain (isnād) upon the condition of the two Shaykhs (al-Bukhārī and Muslim), and narrated by al-Bazzār, and the man was named in one of its narrations as Saʿd — though in it there is Ibn Lahīʿah. (3) The ḥadīth: {«Three things from which no one escapes: suspicion, slander, and envy»} — and in another narration: \"And few are those who escape them.\" It was related by Ibn Abī al-Dunyā in his book \"Dhamm al-Ḥasad\" (The Condemnation of Envy) from the ḥadīth of Abū Hurayrah — though in its chain there are Yaʿqūb ibn Muḥammad al-Zuhrī and Mūsā ibn Yaʿqūb al-Zamaʿī, both of whom the majority of scholars have declared weak. The second narration was also related by Ibn Abī al-Dunyā from the narration of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muʿāwiyah, and it is mursal (broken chain) and weak. Al-Ṭabarānī has something similar from the ḥadīth of Ḥārithah ibn al-Nuʿmān, and it has already preceded in the section on the afflictions of the tongue. And in one narration: {«Three things from which no one escapes — and few are those who escape them»} — so this narration affirms the possibility of deliverance. And he, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: {«There has crept upon you the disease of the nations before you: envy and hatred. And hatred is the clipper — I do not say it clips the hair, but rather it clips the religion. By Him in whose hand is the soul of Muḥammad, you shall not enter Paradise until you believe, and you shall not believe until you love one another. Shall I not inform you of that which will establish this for you? Spread peace (salām) among you»} (4). And he, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: {«Poverty almost becomes unbelief, and envy almost overcomes destiny (qadar)»} (5). And he, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: {«Indeed, my community will be afflicted with the disease of the nations.» They said: \"And what is the disease of the nations?\" He said: {«Extravagance, arrogance, accumulation, rivalry in worldly things, mutual distance, and mutual envy — until transgression (baghy) comes, and then destruction (harj)»} (6). And he, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: {«Do not show gloating (shamātah) over your brother, lest God heal him and afflict you»} (7). And it is narrated that Moses, peace be upon him, when he hastened to his Lord, Exalted is He, saw a man in the shade of the Throne (ʿArsh) and envied him his station (ghabaṭahu), saying: \"This one is honored before his Lord.\" So he asked his Lord, Exalted is He, to inform him of his name, but He did not inform him, and said: \"I shall tell you of three of his deeds: he did not envy people for what God had given them of His bounty, he was not undutiful to his parents, and he did not go about with slander (namīmah).\" And Zechariah, peace be upon him, said: God, Exalted is He, said: {«The envier is an enemy of My blessing, resentful of My decree, dissatisfied with My apportioning which I have apportioned among My servants»}. And he, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: {«The thing I most fear for my community is that wealth will increase among them, and they will envy one another and fight one another»} (1). And he, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: {«Seek help in fulfilling your needs by keeping them secret, for everyone who possesses a blessing is envied»} (2). And he, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: {«Indeed, the blessings of God have enemies.» It was said: \"And who are they?\" He said: {«Those who envy people for what God has given them of His bounty»} (3). The ḥadīth: {«Six will enter the Fire before the Reckoning by a year.» It was said: \"O Messenger of God, who are they?\" He said: {«The unjust rulers»} — the ḥadīth — and in it: {«And the scholars who envy»}. Narrated by Abū Manṣūr al-Daylamī from the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿUmar and Anas with two weak chains of transmission.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Envy", "section_title_ar": "Section 718", "section_title_en": "Section 718 — On Envy (Ḥasad): Its Roots, Branches, and Blameworthy Fruits", "section_index": 718, "anchors": "- Root: حسد (Ḥ-S-D)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of wishing for removal of a blessing from its possessor, rooted in spite and resentment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحسد: تمني زوال نعمة من مستحق لها\" — Envy is the desire for the removal of a blessing from one who rightly deserves it, sometimes accompanied by active striving to effect its removal. The believer envies not, but the hypocrite does.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"حسده على نعمة الله\" — to envy him for God's blessing. Metaphorical (majāz): \"إن الحسد يأكل الجسد\" — envy consumes the body, as fire consumes wood, indicating its destructive spiritual effect.\n- Root: حدث (Ḥ-D-TH)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Origination after non-existence; bringing into being after nothingness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحدوث: كون الشيء بعد أن لم يكن\" — origination is a thing's coming to be after it was not. The ḥadīth (report) is so-called for its temporal proximity to the event it narrates.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"هو حدث من الأحداث\" — he is a youth among youths. Metaphorical: \"صاروا أحاديث\" — they became subjects of talk, indicating fame or infamy spread by tongues.\n- Root: ملك (M-L-K)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Possession, dominion, sovereignty; the one who possesses and disposes.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الملك: هو المتصرف بالأمر والنهي\" — dominion is disposal by command and prohibition. Two kinds: actual possession/sovereignty, and the power/capacity to exercise it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"ملكت فلانة\" — he married her (possessed her in wedlock). Metaphorical: \"ملك نفسه عند الغضب\" — he mastered himself at the moment of anger, i.e., self-restraint.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — When the Prophet (ﷺ) says \"إنما قتل ابن آدم أخاه حين حسده\" (It was only envy that led the son of Adam to kill his brother), the restrictive particle إنما confines the cause of the first murder to envy alone, isolating it as the sole efficient cause of that primordial transgression.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Condemnation of Envy'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "وجل {إنما يتقبل الله من المتقين} ودخل الحسن رضي الله عنه على رجل يجود بنفسه فقال إن أمرا هذا أوله لجدير أن يتقي آخره وإن أمرا هذا آخره لجدير أن يزهد في أوله وقال الجريري كنت عند الجنيد في حال نزعه وكان يوم الجمعة ويوم النيروز وهو يقرأ القرآن فختم فقلت له في الحالة يا أبا القاسم فقال ومن أولى بذلك مني وهو ذا تطوى صحيفتى وقال رويم حضرت وفاة أبي سعيد الخراز وهو يقول حنين قلوب العارفين إلى الذكر .", "english": "When death came to Mu'adh — may God be pleased with him — he said: \"O God, I have indeed feared You, yet today I hope in You. O God, You know that I did not love this world nor long abiding in it for the flowing of rivers, nor for the planting of trees, but rather for the thirst of noonday heat, the endurance of the hours, and the jostling of scholars knee-to-knee at the circles of remembrance.\" And when the agony of death grew severe upon him — a throes such as no one else had endured — every time he regained consciousness from its overwhelming flood, he would open his eyes and then say: \"O Lord, how constricting is Your strangling! By Your might, You know that my heart loves You.\"", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1308", "section_title_en": "The Deathbed Sayings of the Righteous — Yearning for the Beloved at the Final Hour", "section_index": 1308, "anchors": "- Root: موت (m-w-t)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of death as cessation of vital faculty; \"مات ميتة سوء\" — a death of evil kind; \"أماته الله\" — God causes death.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Death (al-mawt) is the cessation of the rational soul's attachment to the body — not annihilation; the state of the dying (naz') is the severing of that attachment.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqiqah) — bodily death; Metaphorical (majaz) — \"أحيا الله البلد الميت\" (reviving dead land), and the death of the heart through heedlessness.\n- Root: بكي (b-k-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Weeping with tears; \"بكاء\" (with prolongation) — weeping with audible wailing; \"بكى\" (with shortening) — weeping where grief predominates over sound.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Weeping is the flow of tears from grief or lamentation; the plural \"بكي\" (bukiyy) describes those overcome by weeping, as in {خروا سجدا وبكيا} (Maryam: 58).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — weeping over the dead; Metaphorical — \"بكت السماء والأرض\" (the sky and earth wept) for the oppressed.\n- Root: فتح (f-t-ḥ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Opening, removing closure; \"فتح الباب\" — opening a door.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Two kinds: (1) perceived by sight — opening a door; (2) perceived by inner vision (basirah) — opening of distress, i.e., removal of grief; also opening the eyes at death as a final glance toward the unseen.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — opening a door; Metaphorical — \"فتح الله عليه: نصره\" (God opened/granted him victory); the opening of the eyes of the dying as a spiritual unveiling.\n- Root: قال (q-w-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Saying, uttering; a comprehensive term for speech, whether statement, question, or command.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — \"إنما يتقبل الله من المتقين\" (Al-Ma'idah: 27): the particle innama restricts acceptance exclusively to the God-fearing, negating any notion of acceptance based on outward deed alone.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Insight"}], "28": [{"arabic": "ظل إلا ظله وعد منهم رجل دعته امرأة ذات جمال وحسب إلى نفسها فقال إني أخاف الله رب العالمين (2) وقصة يوسف عليه السلام وامتناعه من زليخا مع القدرة ومع رغبتها معروفة وقد أثنى الله تعالى عليه بذلك في كتابه العزيز وهو إمام لكل من وفق لمجاهدة الشيطان في هذه الشهوة العظيمة وروي أن سليمان بن يسار كان من أحسن الناس وجها فدخلت عليه امرأة فسألته نفسه فامتنع عليها وخرج هاربا من منزله وتركها فيه قال سليمان فرأيت تلك الليلة في المنام يوسف عليه السلام وكأني أقول له أنت يوسف قال نعم أنا يوسف الذي هممت وأنت سليمان الذي لم تهم أشار إلى قوله", "english": "...which one is ashamed of and fears to plunge into. And the abstention of most people from its requirements is either due to incapacity, or fear, or shame, or concern for preserving one's body — and in none of these is there any reward, for that is merely preferring one share of one's worldly desires over another share. Yes — from divine protection (al-ʿiṣmah) is that one is not able [to sin]; so in these obstacles there is a benefit, namely the repulsion of sin. For whoever abandons fornication, its sin is repelled from him by whatever cause his abandonment occurred. Rather, the virtue and abundant reward lie in abandoning it out of fear of God — may He be exalted — while possessing the ability, with the removal of obstacles, the facilitation of means, and especially at the height of genuine desire. And this is the station of the veracious (al-ṣiddīqīn). For this reason, the Prophet — peace be upon him — said: \"Whoever loves passionately, then remains chaste, then conceals [his love], then dies — he dies a martyr.\"(1)", "book_title": "Harms of the Tongue", "section_title_ar": "Section 654", "section_title_en": "The Book of Breaking the Two Desires — Section 654: On Chastity, Guarding the Gaze, and the Virtue of Restraining Desire", "section_index": 654, "anchors": "- **Root: ع ف ف** (ʿaffa)\n  * **Lisan / Ayn**: Core meaning of abstaining from what is unlawful, refraining, and remaining chaste; al-ʿafāf is the state of one who abstains from forbidden things.\n  * **Al-Raghib (Mufradat)**: Theological nuance — ʿiffah (chastity) is a virtue of the soul that restrains the appetitive faculty from pursuing what is not permissible; it is a station of the ṣiddīqūn (veracious ones).\n  * **Al-Zamakhshari (Asas)**: Literal (ḥaqīqah) — physical abstention from unlawful acts; Metaphorical (majāz) — the heart's abstention from coveting what God has forbidden, which is the higher reality of chastity.\n\n- **Root: غ ب ق** (ghabaqa)\n  * **Lisan / Ayn**: The evening drink (al-ghabūq) — the milking or drink given at nightfall; contrasted with al-ṣabūḥ (the morning drink).\n  * **Al-Raghib (Mufradat)**: In the hadith of the three men in the cave, the term marks the temporal precision of filial devotion — serving parents before any other claim, even at the boundary of night.\n  * **Al-Zamakhshari (Asas)**: Literal — the evening milking; the narrative use in the hadith is literal, denoting the exact act of service delayed out of reverence.\n\n- **Root: ه م م** (hamma)\n  * **Lisan / Ayn**: To intend, to resolve upon a matter; al-hamm is the initial stirring of intention before full determination.\n  * **Al-Raghib (Mufradat)**: In Q. 12:24 {«وَلَقَدْ هَمَّتْ بِهِ وَهَمَّ بِهَا لَوْلَا أَنْ رَأَى بُرْهَانَ رَبِّهِ»} — \"And certainly she desired him, and he would have desired her, had it not been that he saw the proof of his Lord\" — the term distinguishes between a passing inclination (hamm) and a settled resolve; Yūsuf's hamm was repelled by divine proof, while Zulaykhā's was consummated in action.\n  * **Al-Zamakhshari (Asas)**: Literal — the inward movement of intention; the rhetorical force lies in the contrast between the two hamm-s, one punished and one pardoned.\n\n- **Root: ن ظ ر** (naẓara)\n  * **Lisan / Ayn**: To look, to gaze; al-naẓar is the act of sight, whether physical or intellectual.\n  * **Al-Raghib (Mufradat)**: The gaze (naẓar) is the gateway to desire; the Quranic phrase {«يَعْلَمُ خَائِنَةَ الْأَعْيُنِ»} — \"He knows the treachery of the eyes\" (Q. 40:19) — indicates that the look which steals toward the forbidden is itself an act of betrayal recorded by God.\n  * **Al-Zamakhshari (Asas)**: Literal — the first glance that occurs unintentionally; Metaphorical — the second glance is the \"treachery of the eyes,\" for it is a deliberate return to what the first glance stumbled upon by accident.\n\n- **Root: ر و د** (rāwada)\n  * **Lisan / Ayn**: To seek, to desire, to solicit; murāwadah is the act of soliciting someone for an unlawful purpose.\n  * **Al-Raghib (Mufradat)**: In the Yūsuf narrative, murāwadah denotes the persistent solicitation toward sin; the term carries the weight of repeated, deliberate pursuit.\n  * **Al-Zamakhshari (Asas)**: Literal — the physical act of solicitation; the narrative contexts in this section use the term literally for the woman's pursuit of the young man.\n\n- **Sibawayh Rule**: **Bāb al-ḥaṣr wa-l-taqyīd bi-innamā** (Restriction and Focused Predication with innamā)\n  * The particle innamā restricts and confines the predicate to the subject alone, negating all else. In the woman's statement \"innamā urīdu mā yakūnu min al-rajuli ilā ahlihī\" — \"I only desire what comes from a man to his wife\" — the restriction (ḥaṣr) intensifies the gravity of the solicitation, making the young man's resistance all the more virtuous.\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Harms of the Tongue'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "32": [{"arabic": "وقيل فكأنما قتل الناس جميعا وعن علي رضي الله عنه من احتكر الطعام أربعين يوما قسا قلبه وعنه أيضا أنه أحرق طعام محتكر بالنار وروي في فضل ترك الاحتكار عنه صلى الله عليه وسلم من جلب طعاما فباعه بسعر يومه فكأنما تصدق به وفي لفظ آخر فكأنما أعتق رقبة (1) وقيل في قوله تعالى {ومن يرد فيه بإلحاد بظلم نذقه من عذاب أليم} إن الاحتكار من الظلم وداخل تحته في الوعيد وعن بعض السلف أنه كان بواسط فجهز سفينة حنطة إلى البصرة وكتب إلى وكيله بع هذا الطعام يوم يدخل البصرة ولا تؤخره", "english": "The Messenger of Allah, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, said: «من احتكر الطعام أربعين يوما ثم تصدق به لم تكن صدقته كفارة لاحتكاره» — \"Whoever hoards food for forty days and then gives it in charity, his charity shall not be an expiation for his hoarding.\"", "book_title": "Etiquette of Earning", "section_title_ar": "Section 323", "section_title_en": "The First Type: Hoarding (Iḥtikār) — Its Prohibition, Degrees, and Juristic Nuances", "section_index": 323, "anchors": "- Root: حكر (ḥ-k-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of withholding, storing, and monopolizing goods awaiting price inflation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance — hoarding is an act of harm (ḍarar) against the community, tied to greed and distrust of divine provision.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) — physical retention of foodstuffs; Metaphorical (majāz) — hoarding as a spiritual disease of the heart, as in the report of ʿAlī that the hoarder's heart hardens.\n- Root: ضرر (ḍ-r-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Harm, injury, distress — both physical and moral.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ḍarar as the operative criterion (ʿillah) for prohibition; its absence or presence determines the ruling's gradation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — tangible injury to others; Metaphorical — the anticipation of harm (intiẓār mabādiʾ al-ḍarar) treated as equivalent in moral weight to the harm itself.\n- Root: قوت (q-w-t)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Sustenance, staple food — that which maintains life.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Qūt as the fundamental necessity (qiwām) for which creation was sustained; trading in it is disfavored because it seeks profit in essentials.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — grains and staples; Metaphorical — anything that \"stands in the place\" (yasuddu masaddan) of sustenance, extending the ruling analogically.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Taʿlīq al-ḥukm bi-al-ʿillah (Attribution of Ruling to Cause) — The prohibition is anchored to the presence of ḍarar, not to the mere act of storage, allowing graded application across time and genus.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Etiquette of Earning'", "badge": "Afterlife"}], "35": [{"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذلك ذم الله تعالى قوما جعلوا لله ما يكرهون فقال تعال", "english": "The heart is not treated save with the compound electuary (maʿjūn) of knowledge and works. And this condition among the conditions of almsgiving runs in the same course as humility (al-khushūʿ) runs in the prayer. This is established by his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"A man has nothing of his prayer save that of which he was conscious.\" And this is like his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"Allah does not accept the charity of the one who bestows it with reproach (al-mannān).\" And like His saying — Exalted is He: {«لا تبطلوا صدقاتكم بالمن والأذى»} (\"Do not nullify your charities by reproach and injury\"). As for the legal opinion (fatwā) of the jurist that the alms fall in their proper place and that one's liability is discharged without this condition — that is another discussion, and we have already pointed to its meaning in the Book of Prayer.", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Insight"}], "42": [{"arabic": "إليه ولذلك سوى الله عز وجل بين المستمع وأكل السحت فقال تعالى سماعون للكذب أكالون للسحت وقال عز وجل لولا ينهاهم الربانيون والأحبار عن قولهم الإثم وأكلهم السحت فالسكوت على الغيبة حرام وقال تعالى إنكم إذا مثلهم ولذلك قال صلى الله عليه وسلم المغتاب والمستمع شريكان في لإثم (2) الرابع كف بقية الجوارح عن الآثام من اليد والرجل عن المكاره وكف البطن عن الشبهات وقت الإفطار فلا معنى للصوم وهو الكف عن الطعام الحلال ثم الإفطار على الحرام فمثال هذا الصائم مثال من يبني قصرا ويهدم مصرا فإن الطعام الحلال إنما يضر بكثرته لا بنوعه فالصوم لتقليله و", "english": "As for the fast of the elite of the elite, it is the fasting of the heart from base concerns (al-himam al-daniyyah) and worldly thoughts (al-afkār al-dunyawiyyah), and its complete restraint from everything other than God, Mighty and Majestic. The breaking of the fast in this degree occurs through thinking upon other than God, Mighty and Majestic, and the Last Day, and through thinking upon this world — except a worldly matter intended for the sake of religion, for that is of the provisions of the Hereafter and is not of this world. Indeed, the masters of hearts have said: \"Whosoever's aspiration stirs him to act during his day to arrange what he will break his fast upon, a sin is recorded against him\" — for that stems from lack of trust in the bounty of God, Mighty and Majestic, and lack of certainty in His promised provision. This is the station of the prophets, the veracious (al-ṣiddīqīn), and those brought near (al-muqarrabīn). There is no need to prolong the discussion of its details in speech, but rather in actualizing it in deed — for it is the turning of one's whole aspiration toward God, Mighty and Majestic, and the turning away from other than God, Glorified be He, and the clothing of oneself with the meaning of His saying, Mighty and Majestic: {«Say: God — then leave them in their vain play»} (Q. 6:91).", "book_title": "Mysteries of Fasting", "section_title_ar": "Section 188", "section_title_en": "The Three Degrees of Fasting: The Fast of the Common People, the Fast of the Elite, and the Fast of the Elite of the Elite", "section_index": 188, "anchors": "- Root: صوم (ṣ-w-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core linguistic root meaning — \"abstention from action, whether from food, speech, or walking; the horse that refrains from moving or feeding is called ṣā'im\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الصوم في الأصل: الإمساك عن الفعل مطعما كان، أو كلاما، أو مشيا\" — fasting in origin is abstention from any act, whether food, speech, or movement; theological nuance extends to abstention of the heart from all save God\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) — \"شهر الصوم والصيام\" the month of fasting; Metaphorical (Majaz) — \"صام الفرس على آريه إذا لم يعتلف\" the horse fasting upon its stable when it refrains from fodder; hence the heart's fasting from worldly concerns is a metaphorical extension of the same root abstention\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core linguistic root meaning — \"fear, trepidation, the stirring of dread within the breast\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — wajal is the inward sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»} (Q. 8:2) — the believers are those whose hearts tremble when God is mentioned\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" a fearful man, a fearful people; the heart suspended between fear and hope after the fast partakes of this root's semantic field\n- Root: عله (ʿ-l-h)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core linguistic root meaning — \"to occupy, to divert, to preoccupy; the one preoccupied is mashghūl\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The heart's occupation with other than God is the very breaking of the fast of the elite of the elite\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal vs Metaphorical — the heart's being \"occupied\" (mushtaghil) with worldly concerns is the metaphorical breaking of the spiritual fast, just as the stomach's occupation with food breaks the literal fast\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — the tripartite division of fasting (صوم العموم، صوم الخصوص، صوم خصوص الخصوص) employs graded specification, each level restricting and elevating the prior, moving from the corporeal (al-jawāhir) to the immaterial spiritual realities (al-laṭā'if)", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Mysteries of Fasting'", "badge": "Ethics"}], "48": [{"arabic": "عز وجل سقاه الله ومن كسا لله عز وجل كساه الله وقال الحسن لو شاء الله لجعلكم أغنياء لا فقير فيكم ولكنه ابتلى بعضكم ببعض وقال الشعبي من لم ير نفسه إلى ثواب الصدقة أحوج من الفقير إلى صدقته فقد أبطل صدقته وضرب بها وجهه وقال مالك لا نرى بأسا بشرب المؤمن من الماء الذي يتصدق به ويسقى في المسجد لأنه إنما جعل للعطشان من كان ولم يرد به أهل الحاجة والمسكنة على الخصوص ويقال إن الحسن مر به نخاس ومعه جارية فقال للنخاس أترضى في ثمنها الدرهم والدرهمين قال لا قال فاذهب فإن الله عز وجل رضي في الحور العين بالفلس واللقمة", "english": "Among the reports [concerning charity], his saying (peace and blessings be upon him): \"Give charity, even if with a single date-fruit, for it stays the hunger of the hungry and extinguishes sin just as water extinguishes fire.\" (1) And he said (peace and blessings be upon him): \"Guard yourselves against the Fire, even if with half a date-fruit; and if you find not even that, then with a kind word.\" (2) And he said (peace and blessings be upon him): \"There is no Muslim servant who gives charity from lawful earnings — and God accepts only what is lawful — but that God takes it in His Right Hand and nurtures it just as one of you nurtures his foal, until the single date-fruit grows to be like Mount Uḥud.\" (3) And he said (peace and blessings be upon him) to Abū al-Dardāʾ: \"When you cook a broth, increase its water, then look to a household among your neighbors and bestow upon them from it with kindness.\" (4) And he said (peace and blessings be upon him): \"No servant does well in charity but that God (mighty and majestic) does well for him in the stewardship of what he leaves behind.\" (5) And he said (peace and blessings be upon him): \"Every person is in the shade of his charity until judgment is rendered between the people.\" (6) And he said (peace and blessings be upon him): \"Charity closes seventy doors of evil.\" (7) And he said (peace and blessings be upon him): \"Secret charity extinguishes the wrath of the Lord (mighty and majestic).\" And the Messenger of God (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"The one who gives from abundance is not more excellent in reward than the one who accepts from need.\" (8) Perhaps what is meant thereby is the one who intends, by the repelling of his need, to free himself for religion — so that he becomes equal to the giver who intends, by his giving, the flourishing of his religion.", "book_title": "Mysteries of Zakat", "section_title_ar": "Section 181", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Etiquettes of Eating — Section on the Merits of Charity (Ṣadaqah) and Its Etiquettes", "section_index": 181, "anchors": "- Root: صدق (ṣ-d-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of truthfulness, veracity in speech and action; al-Raghib notes its origin is in speech (al-qawl), whether past or future, promise or otherwise.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الصدق والكذب أصلهما في القول\" — truth and falsehood originate in utterance; charity (ṣadaqah) is so named because it is the truthful verification of one's faith through material sacrifice.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"صدقته الحديث\" — to affirm someone's statement as true. Metaphorical (majāz): \"رجل صادق الحملة\" — a man truthful in his charge (i.e., steadfast in battle). Charity is thus the \"truthing\" of one's claim to faith by action.\n- Root: عله (ʿ-l-h) — [Note: This root appears in the scholia but is not directly relevant to the present text; the governing root for \"feeding\" is أ-ك-ل and for \"giving\" is ع-ط-ي]\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear within oneself.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف. يقال: وجل يوجل وجلا، فهو وجل. قال تعالى: {«إِنَّمَا الْمُؤْمِنُونَ الَّذِينَ إِذَا ذُكِرَ اللَّهُ وَجِلَتْ قُلُوبُهُمْ»}\" — the believers are those whose hearts tremble when God is mentioned.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a people fearful. The root conveys the inner trepidation of the heart before divine majesty.\n- Root: سبع (s-b-ʿ)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"أصل السبع العدد\" — the root origin is the number seven; used for the seven heavens, seven earths, and in the present context, the seventy (seventy being a multiple of seven) doors of evil.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"هو سابع سبعة\" — he is the seventh of seven. Metaphorical (majāz): \"أخذه أخذ سبعة\" — \"he seized with the seizure of seven,\" a proverb for intense, overwhelming force. The number seventy thus signifies multiplicity and completeness in repelling evil.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The particle \"innamā\" (إنما) restricts and confines the predicate to the subject alone, negating it for all others. This is crucial in the ḥadīth: \"إنما هي أوساخ الناس\" — charity is exclusively the filth of people, restricted to them and forbidden for the Prophet's family.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Mysteries of Zakat'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "51": [{"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذلك ذم الله تعالى قوما جعلوا لله ما يكرهون فقال تعال", "english": "The heart is not treated save with the compound electuary (maʿjūn) of knowledge and works. And this condition among the conditions of almsgiving runs in the same course as humility (al-khushūʿ) runs in the prayer. This is established by his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"A man has nothing of his prayer save that of which he was conscious.\" And this is like his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"Allah does not accept the charity of the one who bestows it with reproach (al-mannān).\" And like His saying — Exalted is He: {«لا تبطلوا صدقاتكم بالمن والأذى»} (\"Do not nullify your charities by reproach and injury\"). As for the legal opinion (fatwā) of the jurist that the alms fall in their proper place and that one's liability is discharged without this condition — that is another discussion, and we have already pointed to its meaning in the Book of Prayer.", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "من دون المؤمنين وقال تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا لا تتخذوا اليهود والنصارى أولياء وقال تعالى وكذلك نولي بعض الظالمين بعضا وفي الخبر إن الله تعالى أخذ الميثاق على كل مؤمن أن يبغض كل منافق وعلى كل منافق أن يبغض كل مؤمن (3) وقال عليه السلام المرء مع من أحب (4) وقال من أحب قوما ووالاهم حشر معهم يوم القيامة (5) وقال عليه السلام أوثق عرى الإيمان الحب في الله والبغض في الله (6) وشواهد هذا قد ذكرناها في بيان الحب والبغض في الله تعالى من كتاب آداب الصحبة وفي كتاب الأمر بالمعروف والنهي عن المنكر فلا نعيده فإن قلت فقد وردت الآيات و", "english": "...as a partner in his killing. And God—exalted be He—has commanded envy and competition in good things and the warding off of evils, for He said—exalted be He—{وَفِي ذَٰلِكَ فَلْيَتَنَافَسِ الْمُتَنَافِسُونَ} [\"So for this let the competitors compete\"] (Al-Muṭaffifīn 83:26). And the Prophet—peace be upon him—said: \"There is no envy except in two: a man whom God has given wisdom, and he disseminates it among the people and teaches it; and a man whom God has given wealth, and he has been empowered over it to spend it in the truth.\" In another wording: \"And a man whom God has given the Qur'an, and he stands with it through the hours of the night and the day; then a man says: 'If God had given me the like of what He gave this one, I would have done the like of what he does.'\"", "book_title": "Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment", "section_title_ar": "Section 1196", "section_title_en": "On the Reconciliation Between Hatred of Sinners and Satisfaction with Divine Decree — Section 1196", "section_index": 1196, "anchors": "- Root: بغض (b-gh-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning of aversion, repugnance, and the soul's flight from what it disdains; al-baghḍā' as intense enmity.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"البغض: نفار النفس عن الشيء الذي ترغب عنه، وهو ضد الحب\" — hatred is the soul's aversion from what it shuns, the exact opposite of love's attraction; used theologically for God's repudiation of the disobedient.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage denotes personal antipathy; metaphorical (majāz) extension applies to religiously mandated aversion (al-bughḍ fī Allāh) as an act of devotion, not private spite.\n- Root: وجه (w-j-h)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The face as the foremost and noblest part; by extension, an aspect, direction, or respect under which a thing is considered.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"ولما كان الوجه أول ما يستقبلك، وأشرف ما في ظاهر البدن استعمل في مستقبل كل شيء\" — the face is that which first confronts you, hence used for every aspect or angle of consideration.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the bodily face; metaphorical: \"هذا وجه الثوب\" (the face/right side of a garment) — i.e., a facet or dimension of a single act that may be evaluated differently.\n- Root: كره (k-r-h)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Dislike, detestation, and unwillingness; karāhah as the soul's resistance to what befalls it.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Karāhah is the soul's repugnance toward something, distinguished from ridā (satisfaction) which is the soul's acquiescence and embrace.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): The rhetorical key here is that karāhah and ridā are contraries only when they \"تواردا على شيء واحد من جهة واحدة\" — converge on one thing from one single respect; from different respects, no contradiction arises.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The resolution of the apparent contradiction hinges on precise restriction (taqyīd): the act is hated \"من حيث إنه وصف العبد وكسبه\" (insofar as it is the servant's attribute and acquisition) and loved \"من حيث إنه مراد الله\" (insofar as it is God's will and decree). The predicate is qualified by its aspect, eliminating the logical clash.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment'", "badge": "Afterlife"}], "54": [{"arabic": "وإن اشتركوا في مصيبة الحرمان من حقيقة الأمر فالحقيقة فضل الله يؤتيه من يشاء والله ذو الفضل العظيم وحكمته يختص بها من يشاء ومن يؤت الحكمة فقد أوتي خيرا كثيرا ولنعد إلى الغرض فقد أرخينا الطول وطولنا النفس في أمر هو أعلاى من علوم المعاملات التي نقصدها في هذا الكتاب فقد ظهر أن رتبة الهلاك ليس إلا للجهال المكذبين وشهادة ذلك من كتاب الله وسنة رسوله صلى الله عليه وسلم لا تدخل تحت الحصر فلذلك لم نوردها الرتبة الثانية رتبة المعذبين وهذه رتبة من تحلى بأصل الإيمان ولكن قصر في الوفاء بمقتضاه فإن رأس الإيمان هو التوحيد وهو أن لا يعبد", "english": "The fire of Hell is the coldest of fires [in comparison to the fire of separation]. And you should not deny this in the world of the Hereafter, for it has a witnessed parallel in the world of this world. Indeed, one has been seen who was overcome by ecstasy (wajd) and walked upon fire and upon the roots of cane, his feet being cut, yet he did not feel it, due to the extreme dominance of what was in his heart. And you see the angry man, when anger overcomes him in battle, receiving wounds and not feeling them at that moment, because anger is a fire in the heart. The Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, said: {«الغضب قطعة من النار»} \"Anger is a piece of fire.\" The burning of the heart (iḥtirāq al-fu'ād) is more severe than the burning of bodies, and the more severe annuls the sensation of the weaker, as you observe. For destruction from fire and the sword is only inasmuch as they separate between two parts that are linked one to the other by the bond of composition (ta'līf) possible in bodies. So that which separates between the heart and its Beloved, to which it is linked by a bond of composition far more firm than the composition of bodies, is more intensely painful—if you are among the people of insight (abṣār) and the people of hearts. And it is not far-fetched that he who has no heart should not perceive the severity of this pain and should deem it trivial in comparison to bodily pain. For a boy, if he were given the choice between the pain of being deprived of a ball and a polo-stick, and the pain of being deprived of the rank of a Sultan, he would not feel the pain of deprivation from the Sultan's rank at all, nor would he count that as a pain. And the enemy in the field with the polo-stick is more beloved to him than a thousand thrones of the Sultan with sitting upon them. Nay, even one overcome by the lust of the belly, if given the choice between porridge (harīsah) and sweets (ḥalwā) and between a beautiful deed by which he conquers enemies and delights friends, he would prefer the porridge and sweets. All of this is due to the absence of the meaning (maʿnā) by whose existence rank becomes beloved, and the presence of the meaning by whose existence food becomes delicious. This is for one whom the attributes of beasts and predators have enslaved, and in whom the attributes of the angels have not appeared—those attributes which are not suited by, nor delight in, anything but proximity to the Lord of the Worlds, and which are pained only by distance and the veil (ḥijāb). And just as taste is only in the tongue and hearing only in the ears, so this attribute is only in the heart. So he who has no heart has no such sense, like he who has no hearing or sight has no pleasure in melodies or the beauty of forms and colors. And not every human has a heart; were it so, the saying of God, Most High, would not be sound: {«إن في ذلك لذكرى لمن كان له قلب»} \"Indeed in that is a reminder for whoever has a heart\" (Q. 50:37). Thus He made the one who does not heed the Quran devoid of a heart. And by \"heart\" I do not mean this [organ] which the ribs of the chest encompass. Rather, I mean the secret (sirr) which is of the world of the Command (ʿālam al-amr). The flesh which is of the world of Creation (ʿālam al-khalq) is its Throne (ʿarsh), the chest is its Footstool (kursī), and the rest of the limbs are its domain and kingdom. And to God belongs the Creation and the Command altogether. But that secret, concerning which God, Most High, said: {«قل الروح من أمر ربي»} \"Say: The Spirit is of the Command of my Lord\" (Q. 17:85), is the commander and the king, because between the world of the Command and the world of Creation there is an ordering, and the world of the Command is commander over the world of Creation. It is the subtle divine reality (al-laṭīfah) which, when it is sound, the rest of the body is sound for its sake. Whoever knows it has known his own self, and whoever knows his own self has known his Lord. At that point, the servant smells the beginnings of the fragrance of the meaning folded under his saying, peace and blessings be upon him: {«إن الله خلق آدم على صورته»} \"God created Adam upon His image.\" He looks with the eye of mercy upon those who adhere to its literal wording and upon those who are obstinate in the path of its interpretation (ta'wīl). And even though His mercy for those adhering to the letter is greater than His mercy for the obstinate interpreters, because mercy is proportional to the affliction, and the affliction of those [literalists] is greater—even if they share in the affliction of being deprived of the reality of the matter—for the Reality (al-ḥaqīqah) is the bounty of God which He gives to whom He wills, and God is the Possessor of Great Bounty. And His Wisdom is singled out for whom He wills: {«ومن يؤت الحكمة فقد أوتي خيرا كثيرا»} \"And whoever is given wisdom has been given much good\" (Q. 2:269). Let us return to the purpose, for we have drawn out the length and lengthened the breath in a matter that is higher than the sciences of transactions (muʿāmalāt) which we intend in this book. It has become clear that the rank of destruction is only for the ignorant deniers. And the testimony of that from the Book of God and the Sunnah of His Messenger, peace and blessings be upon him, is beyond enumeration, so we have not cited it.", "book_title": "Poverty and Abstinence", "section_title_ar": "Section 930", "section_title_en": "The Ranks of the Hereafter: On the Distribution of Degrees and the States of the Saved, the Punished, and the Victorious", "section_index": 930, "anchors": "- Root: ح ر ق (ḥ-r-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of burning, scorching, and the fire's consumption of its fuel.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The fire of the Hereafter (Nār) is a created reality, but its perception is tied to the spiritual state of the perceiver.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) for physical burning; metaphorical (Majaz) for the burning of the heart (iḥtirāq al-fu'ād) by overwhelming love or anger.\n- Root: ق ل ب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To turn, overturn, or transform a thing from one state to another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The heart (Qalb) is the seat of perception and understanding, distinct from the physical organ.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for the physical heart; metaphorical for the spiritual subtlety (Laṭīfah) that is the locus of divine knowledge and love.\n- Root: ع ل م (ʿ-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To know, to perceive a thing by its true nature.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Knowledge (ʿIlm) is the apprehension of a thing's reality, which is the basis for distinguishing the ranks of the believers.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for acquired knowledge; metaphorical for the \"unveiling\" (Kashf) knowledge granted to the knowers (ʿĀrifūn).\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and Specification (Bāb al-Ḥaṣr wa al-Taqyīd bi-innamā) — Used to distinguish the specific referents of terms like \"heart\" (Qalb) and \"faith\" (Īmān), ensuring the precise meaning is not conflated with common usage.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Poverty and Abstinence'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "لانفضوا من حولك ووصف أولياءه فقال أذلة على المؤمنين أعزة على الكافرين وكذلك قال صلى الله عليه وسلم فيما رواه العباس رضي الله عنه يكون قوم يقرءون القرآن لا يجاوز حناجرهم يقولون قد قرأنا القرآن فمن أقرأ منا ومن أعلم منا ثم التفت إلى أصحابه وقال أولئك منكم أيها الأمة أولئك هم", "english": "The second aspect by which the vice of pride is magnified is that it calls to opposing Allah the Exalted in His commands. For the arrogant man, when he hears the truth from a servant among the servants of Allah, disdains to accept it and girds himself for its denial. For this reason you see the disputants in matters of religion claiming that they are investigating the secrets of the religion, yet they engage in mutual denial—the denial of the arrogant. Whenever the truth becomes clear upon the tongue of one of them, the other refuses to accept it out of pride, girds himself for its denial, and resorts to every stratagem to repel it by whatever obfuscation he can muster. This is among the moral traits of the disbelievers and the hypocrites, for Allah the Exalted described them, saying: {«وَقَالَ الَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا لَا تَسْمَعُوا لِهَذَا الْقُرْآنِ وَالْغَوْا فِيهِ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَغْلِبُونَ»} (And those who disbelieve said: \"Do not listen to this Quran and make noise therein, perhaps you may prevail.\") So whoever disputes for the sake of victory and silencing the opponent—not to seize the truth when it appears to him—has shared with them in this moral trait. Likewise, this pride drives one to refuse admonition, as Allah the Exalted said: {«وَإِذَا قِيلَ لَهُ اتَّقِ اللَّهَ أَخَذَتْهُ الْعِزَّةُ بِالْإِثْمِ»} (And when it is said to him, \"Fear Allah,\" pride in sin seizes him.) It is narrated that ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, recited this verse and then said: {«إِنَّا لِلَّهِ وَإِنَّا إِلَيْهِ رَاجِعُونَ»} (Indeed, we belong to Allah and to Him we return.) A man rose to enjoin good and was killed; then another rose and said: \"They kill those who enjoin justice among the people\"—and the arrogant one who opposed him and the one who commanded him was killed out of pride.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Pride and Conceit", "section_title_ar": "Section 857", "section_title_en": "The Second Aspect by Which the Vice of Pride (Kibr) Is Magnified: That It Leads to Opposing Allah in His Commands", "section_index": 857, "anchors": "- Root: كبر (K-B-R)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of bigness, magnitude, and seniority; al-kibr is the internal disposition of self-magnification.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-Kabīr and al-Ṣaghīr are relative terms (mutadāyifān); kibr as a moral vice is the soul's claim to a station above its due, entailing disdain for others.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage concerns physical or quantitative magnitude; metaphorical (majāz) usage here denotes arrogance and haughtiness—\"كبر عليه\" (he disdained it).\n- Root: علم (ʿ-L-M)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Knowing a thing's reality; the root also carries the sense of a mark/trace (ʿalam) by which one is guided.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): True knowledge (ʿilm) is apprehension of a thing's essence or judgment about it; the highest form is knowledge of Allah, the self, and the Final End—this engenders fear (khashyah).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: perception and awareness; metaphorical: the \"marks\" (maʿālim) of religion—the distinguishing signs of guidance.\n- Root: نفس (N-F-S)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, soul, or spirit; that which is valued and precious.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-Nafs denotes the soul or self; in theological usage it may refer to the essence (dhāt) of a thing.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the soul; metaphorical: self-worth and self-regard—the arrogant man \"magnifies his own soul\" (istaʿẓama nafsahu).\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The verse {إِنَّمَا يَخْشَى اللَّهَ مِنْ عِبَادِهِ الْعُلَمَاءُ} restricts true fear of Allah exclusively to the learned, delimiting the predicate to the subject.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Condemnation of Pride and Conceit'", "badge": "Hadith"}, {"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذلك ذم الله تعالى قوما جعلوا لله ما يكرهون فقال تعال", "english": "The heart is not treated save with the compound electuary (maʿjūn) of knowledge and works. And this condition among the conditions of almsgiving runs in the same course as humility (al-khushūʿ) runs in the prayer. This is established by his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"A man has nothing of his prayer save that of which he was conscious.\" And this is like his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"Allah does not accept the charity of the one who bestows it with reproach (al-mannān).\" And like His saying — Exalted is He: {«لا تبطلوا صدقاتكم بالمن والأذى»} (\"Do not nullify your charities by reproach and injury\"). As for the legal opinion (fatwā) of the jurist that the alms fall in their proper place and that one's liability is discharged without this condition — that is another discussion, and we have already pointed to its meaning in the Book of Prayer.", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "فقال صلى الله عليه وسلم ذلك فضل الله يؤتيه من يشاء (1) وقد استشهد ابن عطاء أيضا لما سئل عن ذلك فقال الغني أفضل لأنه وصف الحق أما دليله الأول ففيه نظر لأن الخبر قد ورد مفصلا تفصيلا يدل على خلاف ذلك وهو أن ثواب الفقير في التسبيح يزيد على ثواب الغني وأن فوزهم بذلك الثواب فضل الله يؤتيه من يشاء فقد روى زيد بن أسلم عن أنس بن مالك رضي الله عنه قال بعث الفقراء رسولا إلا رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم فقال إني رسول الفقراء إليك فقال {مرحبا} بك وبمن جئت من عندهم قوم أحبهم قال قالوا يا رسول الله إن", "english": "Evidence for this may be found in what is related in the report (khabar) that the poor complained to the Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, that the rich had preceded them in good deeds, alms, pilgrimage (ḥajj), and holy struggle (jihād). So he taught them words of glorification (tasbīḥ) and told them that by these they would attain what surpassed what the rich had attained. Then the rich learned these words and began to say them, so the poor returned to the Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, and informed him [of this]. He said, peace and blessings be upon him: {«ذلك فضل الله يؤتيه من يشاء»} — \"That is the bounty of God; He gives it to whom He wills.\" (1)", "book_title": "Poverty and Abstinence", "section_title_ar": "Section 1074", "section_title_en": "On the Relative Excellence of Poverty and Wealth — A Detailed Exposition", "section_index": 1074, "anchors": "- Root: فقر (fa-qā-rā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of neediness, destitution; the state of one lacking provision or means.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Fourfold division — (1) essential need before God (universal to all creatures), (2) lack of acquired possessions, (3) contentment of the soul, (4) metaphorical usage. Here, the operative sense is the second: absence of worldly goods.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) — absence of wealth; Metaphorical (Majaz) — \"فقرة الكلام\" (a rhetorical gem/point), showing the root's capacity for extension. The discussion here remains in the literal register of material need.\n- Root: غني (ghayn-nūn-yā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of sufficiency, independence, freedom from need.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Threefold division — (1) absolute freedom from all needs (God alone), (2) fewness of needs (the praised \"wealth of the soul\"), (3) abundance of possessions. The debate here pivots between senses (2) and (3).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — \"أغنى عنه\" (to be independent of); Metaphorical — \"تغنته القيود\" (fetters \"enriched\" him, i.e., clung to him), showing that attachment, not mere possession, is the real issue.\n- Root: صبر (ṣād-bā-rā)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Restraint of the soul in the face of hardship; steadfastness in obedience and abstinence from sin. The \"patient poor\" (فقير صابر) is the one who holds fast without anxious striving.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — endurance of affliction; the compound \"صابر محتسب\" (patient and reckoning on God's reward) is the technical Quranic-Hadith register.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The Prophet's saying {«ليس الغنى عن كثرة العرض إنما الغنى غنى النفس»} uses \"إنما\" to restrict true wealth to soul-contentment, negating the apparent sense of abundance. This syntactic restriction governs the entire argument: the \"wealth\" that matters is a state of the heart, not of the purse.", "content_type": "dhikr", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on constant remembrance of God and contemplative invocations, from 'Poverty and Abstinence'", "badge": "Dhikr"}], "57": [{"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذلك ذم الله تعالى قوما جعلوا لله ما يكرهون فقال تعال", "english": "The heart is not treated save with the compound electuary (maʿjūn) of knowledge and works. And this condition among the conditions of almsgiving runs in the same course as humility (al-khushūʿ) runs in the prayer. This is established by his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"A man has nothing of his prayer save that of which he was conscious.\" And this is like his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"Allah does not accept the charity of the one who bestows it with reproach (al-mannān).\" And like His saying — Exalted is He: {«لا تبطلوا صدقاتكم بالمن والأذى»} (\"Do not nullify your charities by reproach and injury\"). As for the legal opinion (fatwā) of the jurist that the alms fall in their proper place and that one's liability is discharged without this condition — that is another discussion, and we have already pointed to its meaning in the Book of Prayer.", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Insight"}], "63": [{"arabic": "للكذب أكالون للسحت وقال عز وجل لولا ينهاهم الربانيون والأحبار عن قولهم الإثم وأكلهم السحت فالسكوت على الغيبة حرام وقال تعالى إنكم إذا مثلهم ولذلك قال صلى الله عليه وسلم المغتاب والمستمع شريكان في لإثم (2) الرابع كف بقية الجوارح عن الآثام من اليد والرجل عن المكاره وكف البطن عن الشبهات وقت الإفطار فلا معنى للصوم وهو الكف عن الطعام الحلال ثم الإفطار على الحرام فمثال هذا الصائم مثال من يبني قصرا ويهدم مصرا فإن الطعام الحلال إنما يضر بكثرته لا بنوعه فالصوم لتقليله وتارك الاستكثار من الدواء خوفا من ضرره إذا عدل إلى تناول السم كان سفيها و", "english": "As for the fast of the elite of the elite, it is the fasting of the heart from base concerns (al-himam al-daniyyah) and worldly thoughts (al-afkār al-dunyawiyyah), and its complete restraint from everything other than God, Mighty and Majestic. The breaking of the fast in this degree occurs through thinking upon other than God, Mighty and Majestic, and the Last Day, and through thinking upon this world — except a worldly matter intended for the sake of religion, for that is of the provisions of the Hereafter and is not of this world. Indeed, the masters of hearts have said: \"Whosoever's aspiration stirs him to act during his day to arrange what he will break his fast upon, a sin is recorded against him\" — for that stems from lack of trust in the bounty of God, Mighty and Majestic, and lack of certainty in His promised provision. This is the station of the prophets, the veracious (al-ṣiddīqīn), and those brought near (al-muqarrabīn). There is no need to prolong the discussion of its details in speech, but rather in actualizing it in deed — for it is the turning of one's whole aspiration toward God, Mighty and Majestic, and the turning away from other than God, Glorified be He, and the clothing of oneself with the meaning of His saying, Mighty and Majestic: {«Say: God — then leave them in their vain play»} (Q. 6:91).", "book_title": "Mysteries of Fasting", "section_title_ar": "Section 188", "section_title_en": "The Three Degrees of Fasting: The Fast of the Common People, the Fast of the Elite, and the Fast of the Elite of the Elite", "section_index": 188, "anchors": "- Root: صوم (ṣ-w-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core linguistic root meaning — \"abstention from action, whether from food, speech, or walking; the horse that refrains from moving or feeding is called ṣā'im\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الصوم في الأصل: الإمساك عن الفعل مطعما كان، أو كلاما، أو مشيا\" — fasting in origin is abstention from any act, whether food, speech, or movement; theological nuance extends to abstention of the heart from all save God\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) — \"شهر الصوم والصيام\" the month of fasting; Metaphorical (Majaz) — \"صام الفرس على آريه إذا لم يعتلف\" the horse fasting upon its stable when it refrains from fodder; hence the heart's fasting from worldly concerns is a metaphorical extension of the same root abstention\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core linguistic root meaning — \"fear, trepidation, the stirring of dread within the breast\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — wajal is the inward sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»} (Q. 8:2) — the believers are those whose hearts tremble when God is mentioned\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" a fearful man, a fearful people; the heart suspended between fear and hope after the fast partakes of this root's semantic field\n- Root: عله (ʿ-l-h)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core linguistic root meaning — \"to occupy, to divert, to preoccupy; the one preoccupied is mashghūl\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The heart's occupation with other than God is the very breaking of the fast of the elite of the elite\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal vs Metaphorical — the heart's being \"occupied\" (mushtaghil) with worldly concerns is the metaphorical breaking of the spiritual fast, just as the stomach's occupation with food breaks the literal fast\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — the tripartite division of fasting (صوم العموم، صوم الخصوص، صوم خصوص الخصوص) employs graded specification, each level restricting and elevating the prior, moving from the corporeal (al-jawāhir) to the immaterial spiritual realities (al-laṭā'if)", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Mysteries of Fasting'", "badge": "Ethics"}, {"arabic": "ينهاهم الربانيون والأحبار عن قولهم الإثم وأكلهم السحت لبئس ما كانوا يصنعون فبين أنهم أثموا بترك النهي وقال تعالى فلولا كان من القرون من قبلكم أولوا بقية ينهون عن الفساد في الأرض الآية فبين أنه أهلك جميعهم إلا قليلا منهم كانوا ينهون عن الفساد وقال تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا كونوا قوامين بالقسط شهداء لله ولو على أنفسكم أو الوالدين والأقربين وذلك هو الأمر بالمعروف للوالدين والأقربين وقال تعالى لا خير في كثير من نجواهم إلا من أمر بصدقة أو معروف أو إصلاح بين الناس ومن يفعل ذلك ابتغاء مرضاة الله فسوف نؤتيه أجرا عظيما وقال تعالى وإن طائف", "english": "Know that the verses indicating the obligation of commanding right and forbidding wrong are numerous. Among them is His saying, may He be exalted: {«Let there arise from among you a nation inviting to good, commanding right and forbidding wrong — and those are the successful»} (Āl ʿImrān 3:104). In this verse is the declaration of obligation, for His saying, may He be exalted, \"Let there be\" (wal-takun) is a command, and the apparent meaning of a command is obligation. In it also is the declaration that success (falāḥ) is contingent upon it, for He restricted it and said: {«And those are the successful»}. In it also is the declaration that it is a collective obligation (farḍ kifāyah), not an individual obligation (farḍ ʿayn), and that when a group undertakes it, the obligation falls from the rest — for He did not say, \"Let all of you be commanders of right,\" but rather said: {«Let there arise from among you a nation»}. Thus, whenever one person or a group undertakes it, the burden falls from the others, and success is particularized to those who undertake it and directly perform it. But if all of creation were to hold back from it, the burden would encompass all those capable of it without exception.", "book_title": "Enjoining Good and Forbidding Evil", "section_title_ar": "Section 521", "section_title_en": "Section 521", "section_index": 521, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Enjoining Good and Forbidding Evil'", "badge": "Insight"}], "69": [{"arabic": "الشك في هذا الصدق وكذلك قال الله تعالى ولكن البر من آمن بالله واليوم الآخر والملائكة والكتاب والنبيين فشرط عشرين وصفا كالوفاء بالعهد والصبر على الشدائد ثم قال تعالى أولئك الذين صدقوا وقد قال تعالى يرفع الله الذين آمنوا منكم والذين أوتوا العلم درجات وقال تعالى لا يستوي منكم من أنفق من قبل الفتح وقاتل الآية وقد قال تعالى هم درجات عند الله وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم الإيمان عريان ولباسه التقوى (1) الحديث وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم الإيمان بضع وسبعون بابا أدناها إماطة الأذى عن الطريق فهذا ما يدل على ارتباط كمال الإيمان بالأعمال وأ", "english": "...doubt concerning this truthfulness. Likewise, God Most High said: {«But righteousness is he who believes in God and the Last Day and the angels and the Book and the prophets»} (2:177) — thus He conditioned twenty attributes, such as faithfulness to covenant and patience under hardship. Then He said Most High: {«Those are the truthful ones»} (2:177). And God Most High said: {«God will raise those who have believed among you and those who have been given knowledge, by degrees»} (58:11). And He said Most High: {«Not equal among you are those who spent before the conquest and fought»} (57:10). And He said Most High: {«They are degrees before God»} (3:163). And the Prophet, peace be upon him, said: \"Faith is naked, and its garment is God-consciousness (al-taqwā).\" And the Prophet, peace be upon him, said: \"Faith has seventy-some branches; the lowest of them is removing harm from the path.\" All this indicates the interconnection of the perfection of faith with works.", "book_title": "Monitoring and Accounting", "section_title_ar": "Section 104", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Book of the Secrets of Zakāh, Section 104: On the Degrees of Faith and Its Connection to Works, and the Hidden Hypocrisy (al-Nifāq al-Khafī) That Contradicts the Perfection of Faith", "section_index": 104, "anchors": "- Root: ن ف ق (N-F-Q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Root denotes passing away, depletion, and expenditure; also the underground burrow of the jerboa (nāfiqāʾ) with a concealed exit — the etymological basis for hypocrisy as a hidden exit from faith.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"نفق الشيء: مضى ونفد\" — theological nuance: hypocrisy (nifāq) is that which causes faith to expire and pass away, whether by sale (worldly gain) or by death (spiritual ruin).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"نفقت الدراهم\" (coins ran out). Metaphorical: \"نفق روحه: خرج\" (his soul expired and went forth) — hence the hypocrite's faith is one whose spirit has already departed.\n- Root: أ ي م (A-Y-M)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A woman without a husband; a man long unmarried. Al-Raghib: \"الحرب مأيمة\" — war makes women widows.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"تركوا النساء أيامى\" (they left women widowed). Metaphorical application: the one who feels secure from hypocrisy is like one who claims immunity from widowhood — a false security.\n- Root: و ف ي (W-F-Y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Fullness, completion, fulfillment of a measure or covenant.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوافي: الذي بلغ التمام\" — the one who has reached completion. \"أوفى: إذا تمم العهد ولم ينقض حفظه\" — to fulfill the covenant without breach.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"درهم واف\" (a full-weight dirham). Metaphorical: \"أوفى على المائة\" (he surpassed a hundred) — faith's perfection (wafāʾ) is only attained by surpassing mere profession to include works and inward sincerity.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the grammatical force of \"إنما\" constructions and conditional clauses (من كان...), where the predicate is restricted to the condition stated, as in the hadith \"أربع من كن فيه فهو منافق خالص\" — the four traits are exhaustive of the ruling.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Monitoring and Accounting'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "72": [{"arabic": "فلم يحطها بالنصيحة حرم الله عليه الجنة (1) ويقول إني ربما قبضت من عطائهم إرادة صلاحهم واستصلاحهم وأن يرجعوا إلى طاعتهم فيبلغ أمير المؤمنين أني قبضتها على ذلك النحو فيكتب إلى أن لا ترده فلا أستطيع رد أمره ولا أستطيع إنفاذ كتابه وحق الله ألزم من حق أمير المؤمنين والله أحق أن يطاع ولا طاعة لمخلوق في معصية الخالق فأعرض كتاب أمير المؤمنين على كتاب الله عز وجل فإن وجدته موافقا لكتاب الله فخذ به وإن وجدته مخالفا لكتاب الله فانبذه يا ابن هبيرة اتق الله فإنه يوشك أن يأتيك رسول من رب العالمين يزيلك", "english": "He turned to al-Shaʿbī and said: \"O Abā ʿAmr! I am the trustee of the Commander of the Faithful over Iraq and his governor over it, and a man commanded to obedience. I have been tried with the subjects, and their right has become incumbent upon me. So I love to preserve them and to attend to what sets them right, along with sincere counsel (naṣīḥah) to them. And reports may reach me about a band of people from the regions concerning a matter, and I find myself severe against them therein, so I withhold a portion of their stipends and place it in the treasury (bayt al-māl), with the intention in myself that I shall return it to them. Then it reaches the Commander of the Faithful that I have withheld it in that manner, so he writes to me that I must not return it. I cannot then countermand his order, nor can I execute his letter [against my better judgment]. I am but a man commanded to obedience. Is there any liability upon me in this, and in similar matters, where the intention is as I have described?\"", "book_title": "Enjoining Good and Forbidding Evil", "section_title_ar": "Section 553", "section_title_en": "The Upright Scholars Before the Tyrannical Rulers — Counsel and Fearless Truth-Telling", "section_index": 553, "anchors": "- Root: نصح (naṣaḥa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is sincerity, faithfulness, and pure counsel free from deceit; the root denotes the act of seeking the welfare of another with complete integrity.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological context, naṣīḥah is the obligation of the sincere advisor to guide the ruler toward divine truth, even at personal peril, as it is a form of worship (ʿibādah) and a trust (amānah).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage denotes pure honey (ʿasal naṣīḥ) free from admixture; metaphorical (majāz) usage applies to counsel that is untainted by flattery or self-interest.\n- Root: سلط (sulṭ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Denotes authority, power, and dominion; the ruler (sulṭān) is one who has been given sway over affairs.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In Kalam, sulṭān is a trial (ibtilāʾ) and a trust (amānah) from God, whose exercise demands justice (ʿadl) and whose neglect invites divine wrath.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal usage refers to physical power or a governing authority; metaphorical usage extends to the authority of proof (sulṭān al-burhān) that compels assent.\n- Root: عظ (waʿaẓa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Root denotes admonition, exhortation, and reminding of consequences; it implies softening the heart through remembrance of God and the Hereafter.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological terms, ʿiẓah is the act of turning the soul away from transgression through a blend of fear (tarhīb) and hope (targhīb), grounded in revealed truth.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal usage is the verbal admonition; metaphorical usage is the admonition of circumstance (ʿiẓah al-ḥāl) when events themselves teach a lesson.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Restriction and Focused Predication (bāb al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd bi-innamā) — Governing the emphasis of exclusive predication in statements of counsel, such as \"No obedience to a creature in disobedience of the Creator,\" where the restriction (ḥaṣr) is achieved through negation and exception (nafy wa istithnāʾ).", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Enjoining Good and Forbidding Evil'", "badge": "Afterlife"}], "78": [{"arabic": "المؤمنين المنعوتين في هذه الآية وقال تعالى لعن الذين كفروا من بني إسرائيل على لسان داود وعيسى ابن مريم ذلك بما عصوا وكانوا يعتدون كانوا لا يتناهون عن منكر فعلوه لبئس ما كانوا يفعلون وهذا غاية التشديد إذ علل استحقاقهم للعنة بتركهم النهي عن المنكر وقال عز وجل كنتم خير أمة أخرجت للناس تأمرون بالمعروف وتنهون عن المنكر وهذا يدل على فضيلة الأمر بالمعروف والنهي عن المنكر إذ بين أنهم كانوا به خير أمة أخرجت للناس وقال تعالى فلما نسوا ما ذكروا به أنجينا الذين ينهون عن السوء وأخذنا الذين ظلموا بعذاب بئيس بما كانوا يفسقون فبين أنهم استفادوا النجاة بالنهي عن السوء ويدل ذلك على الوجوب أيضا وقال تعالى الذين إن مكناهم في الأرض أقاموا الصلاة وآتوا", "english": "Know that the verses indicating the obligation of commanding right and forbidding wrong are numerous. Among them is His saying, may He be exalted: {«Let there arise from among you a nation inviting to good, commanding right and forbidding wrong — and those are the successful»} (Āl ʿImrān 3:104). In this verse is the declaration of obligation, for His saying, may He be exalted, \"Let there be\" (wal-takun) is a command, and the apparent meaning of a command is obligation. In it also is the declaration that success (falāḥ) is contingent upon it, for He restricted it and said: {«And those are the successful»}. In it also is the declaration that it is a collective obligation (farḍ kifāyah), not an individual obligation (farḍ ʿayn), and that when a group undertakes it, the obligation falls from the rest — for He did not say, \"Let all of you be commanders of right,\" but rather said: {«Let there arise from among you a nation»}. Thus, whenever one person or a group undertakes it, the burden falls from the others, and success is particularized to those who undertake it and directly perform it. But if all of creation were to hold back from it, the burden would encompass all those capable of it without exception.", "book_title": "Enjoining Good and Forbidding Evil", "section_title_ar": "Section 521", "section_title_en": "Section 521", "section_index": 521, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Enjoining Good and Forbidding Evil'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "إسرائيل على لسان داود وعيسى ابن مريم ذلك بما عصوا وكانوا يعتدون كانوا لا يتناهون عن منكر فعلوه لبئس ما كانوا يفعلون وهذا غاية التشديد إذ علل استحقاقهم للعنة بتركهم النهي عن المنكر وقال عز وجل كنتم خير أمة أخرجت للناس تأمرون بالمعروف وتنهون عن المنكر وهذا يدل على فضيلة الأمر بالمعروف والنهي عن المنكر إذ بين أنهم كانوا به خير أمة أخرجت للناس وقال تعالى فلما نسوا ما ذكروا به أنجينا الذين ينهون عن السوء وأخذنا الذين ظلموا بعذاب بئيس بما كانوا يفسقون فبين أنهم استفادوا النجاة بالنهي عن السو", "english": "Know that the verses indicating the obligation of commanding right and forbidding wrong are numerous. Among them is His saying, may He be exalted: {«Let there arise from among you a nation inviting to good, commanding right and forbidding wrong — and those are the successful»} (Āl ʿImrān 3:104). In this verse is the declaration of obligation, for His saying, may He be exalted, \"Let there be\" (wal-takun) is a command, and the apparent meaning of a command is obligation. In it also is the declaration that success (falāḥ) is contingent upon it, for He restricted it and said: {«And those are the successful»}. In it also is the declaration that it is a collective obligation (farḍ kifāyah), not an individual obligation (farḍ ʿayn), and that when a group undertakes it, the obligation falls from the rest — for He did not say, \"Let all of you be commanders of right,\" but rather said: {«Let there arise from among you a nation»}. Thus, whenever one person or a group undertakes it, the burden falls from the others, and success is particularized to those who undertake it and directly perform it. But if all of creation were to hold back from it, the burden would encompass all those capable of it without exception.", "book_title": "The Lawful and Prohibited", "section_title_ar": "Section 521", "section_title_en": "Section 521", "section_index": 521, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'The Lawful and Prohibited'", "badge": "Insight"}], "82": [{"arabic": "الشاقة طمعا في الآخرة وأثنى الله عز وجل عليهم في كتابه فقال {ذلك بأن منهم قسيسين ورهبانا وأنهم لا يستكبرون} فلما اندرس ذلك وأقبل الخلق على اتباع الشهوات وهجروا التجرد لعبادة الله عز وجل وفتروا عنه بعث الله عز وجل نبيه محمدا صلى الله عليه وسلم لإحياء طريق الآخرة وتجديد سنة المرسلين في سلوكها فسأله أهل الملل عن الرهبانية والسياحة في دينه فقال صلى الله عليه وسلم أبدلنا الله بها الجهاد والتكبير على كل شرف (1) يعني الحج وسئل صلى الله عليه وسلم عن السائحين فقال هم الصائمون (2) فأنعم الله عز وجل على هذه الأمة بأن جعل الحج رهبانية لهم فشرف البيت العتيق بالإض", "english": "For this reason, the monastics (al-ruhbāniyyūn) in the past religions separated themselves from creation and withdrew to the peaks of mountains, preferring wild solitude away from people in pursuit of intimacy (uns) with God — mighty and majestic is He. They abandoned for God's sake the pleasures of the present and imposed upon themselves arduous spiritual exertions (mujāhidāt), hoping for the Hereafter. God — glorified and exalted is He — praised them in His Book, saying: {«ذلك بأن منهم قسيسين ورهبانا وأنهم لا يستكبرون»} — \"That is because among them are priests and monks and because they do not behave arrogantly.\" Then when that [way] became effaced, and creation turned to following appetites, abandoned devotion to the worship of God — glorified and exalted is He — and grew languid in it, God — glorified and exalted is He — sent His Prophet Muhammad — may God's peace and blessings be upon him — to revive the path of the Hereafter and renew the way of the messengers in traversing it.", "book_title": "Mysteries of Hajj", "section_title_ar": "Section 216", "section_title_en": "The Noble Stations of Pilgrimage and the Manner of Contemplation Upon Them — From the Beginning of Hajj to Its End", "section_index": 216, "anchors": "- Root: فهم (f-h-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Comprehension, understanding, grasping the reality of a thing\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Intellectual apprehension that penetrates to the essential meaning, not mere surface awareness\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — understanding speech; Metaphorical — understanding the inner reality and wisdom of sacred acts\n- Root: سفر (s-f-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Journey; also uncovering/disclosure — the traveler is one who uncovers distant horizons\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"السفر: كشف الغطاء\" — uncovering the veil; the journey of hajj is both physical travel and spiritual unveiling\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — journeying across land; Metaphorical — \"سفر عن وجهك الشر\" (uncovering evil from your face), i.e., the hajj journey unveils the secrets of servitude\n- Root: رهب (r-h-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To fear, to be awed; monastic withdrawal (rahbāniyyah) from worldly attachments\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Fear that drives one to solitary devotion; the monastic impulse redirected by Islamic law\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — fear; Metaphorical — the disciplined retreat from worldly pleasures for divine communion\n- Root: بيت (b-y-t)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Place of nightly habitation; the Ka'bah as the primordial house\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"أصل البيت: مأوى الإنسان بالليل\" — the house is where one dwells at night; the Sacred House is where hearts find nocturnal rest in God\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — physical structure; Metaphorical — the locus of divine presence and the object of spiritual yearning\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — \"إنما\" restricts the predicate exclusively to the subject, as in \"إنما بعث الله نبيه لإحياء طريق الآخرة\" — the sole purpose of prophetic mission is specified with exclusive focus.", "content_type": "dhikr", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on constant remembrance of God and contemplative invocations, from 'Mysteries of Hajj'", "badge": "Dhikr"}], "83": [{"arabic": "وقد أثنى الله تعالى على أهل الوجد بالقرآن فقال تعالى {وإذا سمعوا ما أنزل إلى الرسول ترى أعينهم تفيض من الدمع مما عرفوا من الحق} وروي أن رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم كان يصلي ولصدره أزيز كأزيز المرجل (7) وأما ما نقل من الوجد بالقرآن عن الصحابة رضي الله عنهم والتابعين فكثير فمنهم من صعق ومنهم من بكى ومنهم من غشي عليه ومنهم من مات في غشيته وروى أن زرارة بن أوفى وكان من التابعين كان يؤم الناس بالرقة فقرأ {فإذا نقر في الناقور} فصعق ومات في محرابه رحمه الله وسمع عمر رضي الله عنه رجلا يقرأ إن عذاب ربك لواقع ماله من دافع فصاح صيحة وخر مغشيا عليه فحمل إلى بيته فلم يزل مريضا في بي", "english": "**QUAD_ANCHORS:**\n- Root: وجد (Wajd)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root وجد encompasses multiple senses: finding/attaining (إدراك), intense love (الحب الشديد), grief (الحزن), and wealth/sufficiency (الغنى). Al-Khalil notes the range: \"الوجد: الحب. والوجد: الحزن. والوجد: الغنى. والوجد: إدراك الشيء\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجد: إدراك الشيء على خلاف ما يطلب\" — and in the technical terminology of the Sufis: \"ما يصادف القلب من واردات الأحوال\" (what the heart encounters of the influxes of spiritual states)\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal sense is finding/attaining; metaphorical extension (majāz) in devotional context: \"وجد فلان في العبادة وجدا: إذا أتى بها على وجهها مع حضور القلب\"\n- Root: طبع (Tabʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الطبع: السجية التي جبل عليها الإنسان\" — the innate disposition upon which one is created; also \"الختم\" (sealing)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الطبع: السجية التي جبل عليها الإنسان\" — the original constitution\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Distinguishes \"مطبوع\" (innate/natural) from \"متكلف\" (deliberately acquired) — \"فلان مطبوع على الكرم، ومتكلف له\"\n- Root: كلف (Kulfah/Takalluf)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"التكلف: تجشم ما لا يلزمك\" — undertaking what is not incumbent upon you with effort and hardship\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"التكلف: تجشم ما ليس من شأنه\" — burdening oneself with what is not one's natural concern\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"تكلفت الأمر: تجشمته على مشقة\" — deliberate self-imposition of effort\n- Root: سمع (Samāʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Auditory perception; also what is heard — recitation, song, remembrance\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"السماع عند الصوفية: ما يهيج الوجد من الأصوات الموزونة\" — that which stirs ecstatic finding from measured sounds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"السماع عند القوم: حضور مجلس يهيج الأحوال الشريفة\" — attending an assembly that stirs noble spiritual states\n- Root: عشق (ʿIshq)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"العشق: فرط الحب\" — excessive love; from \"العشقة\" — a plant that twines around another\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العشق: فرط الحب، وأصله من عشقة النبات وهي ما التف منه على غيره\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"العشق مجاز في حق الله تعالى\" — metaphorical when applied to God\n- Root: بكى (Bukāʾ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Weeping, shedding tears\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"البكاء: إجراء الدمع من عين لحزن أو غيره\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"تباكى: تكلف البكاء\" — deliberate self-induced weeping\n- Root: صعق (Ṣaʿq)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الصعق: الغشي. والصعق: الموت\" — swooning/loss of consciousness; death\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الصعق: غشية تعرض من صوت شديد أو هول\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"صعق من هول الأمر: إذا غشي عليه من شدة الخوف\"\n- Root: خشع (Khushūʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الخشوع: الخضوع والتذلل\" — humility and submissiveness\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الخشوع: التذلل والخضوع، وقيل: الخشوع في الصوت والبصر، والخضوع في البدن\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"خشعت الأصوات: سكنت. وخشع البصر: انكسر\"\n- Root: أنس (Uns)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الأنس: نقيض الوحشة\" — the opposite of loneliness/estrangement\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الأنس: حضور القلب مع المحبوب\" — the heart's presence with the Beloved\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"الأنس بالله: سكون القلب إليه\"\n- Root: وجل (Wajal)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الوجل: الخوف مع اضطراب القلب\" — fear accompanied by cardiac agitation\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: الخوف مع اضطراب القلب\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"الوجل: أشد من الخوف، فإنه مع اضطراب\"\n- Root: طمأن (Ṭumaʾnīnah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الطمأنينة: السكون\" — tranquility\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الطمأنينة: سكون القلب بعد اضطراب\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"اطمأن القلب: سكن بعد اضطراب\"\n- Root: قشعر (Iqshirʿār)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"القشعريرة: تقبض الجلد من الخوف\" — contraction of the skin from fear\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الاقشعرار: انقباض الجلد وتقبضه من الخوف\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"اقشعر الجلد من الخوف: إذا تقبض\"\n- Root: لين (Līn)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"اللين: ضد الخشونة\" — softness, opposite of hardness\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"اللين: نقيض الخشونة\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"لان قلبه: إذا سكن وخشع\"\n- Root: غشي (Ghashy)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الغشي: الإغماء\" — loss of consciousness\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الغشية: ما يغشى القلب من شدة الوجد\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"غشي عليه: إذا أغمي عليه من شدة وجد أو خوف\"\n- Root: زعق (Zaʿq)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الزعق: الصياح الشديد\" — violent screaming\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الزعق: الصياح الشديد\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"زعق: صاح بأعلى صوته\"\n- Root: أزيز (Azīz)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الأزيز: صوت النار والمرجل\" — the sound of fire and boiling pot\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الأزيز: صوت النار والمرجل ونحوهما\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"أزيز الصدر: صوت يسمع من شدة البكاء والوجد\"\n- Root: شهق (Shahīq)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الشهيق: رد النفس إلى الداخل بصوت\" — drawing breath inward with sound\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الشهيق: رد النفس إلى الداخل بصوت\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"شهق الرجل: إذا خرجت نفسه بصوت من شدة الوجد\"\n- Root: حزن (Ḥuzn)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الحزن: ضد السرور\" — grief, opposite of joy\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الحزن: ألم النفس\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"الحزن: ألم النفس، وهو مجاز من حزن الأرض\" (metaphorical extension from rough ground)\n- Root: شيب (Shayb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الشيب: بياض الشعر\" — whitening of hair\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الشيب: بياض الشعر\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"شيبتني هود: أي: كبرتني وأحزنتني حتى شاب شعري\"\n- Root: ذرف (Dharf)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الذرف: سيلان الدمع\" — flowing of tears\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"ذرفت العين: سال دمعها\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"ذرفت عينه: إذا سال دمعها من حزن\"\n- Root: فيض (Fayḍ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الفيض: سيلان الماء\" — overflowing of water\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الفيض: جريان الماء بكثرة\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"فاضت العين بالدمع: سالت. وفاض القلب: امتلأ\"\n- Root: نقر (Naqr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"النقر: الضرب على الشيء حتى يصوت\" — striking something until it sounds\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الناقور: الصور\" — the Trumpet\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"الناقور: الصور الذي ينفخ فيه إسرافيل\"\n- Root: رعش (Riʿshah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الرعدة: الاضطراب\" — trembling\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الارتعداد: الاضطراب\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"ارتعدت فرائصه: اضطربت من شدة الخوف\"\n- Root: فرص (Farīṣah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الفريصة: لحمة بين الجنب والكتف ترعد عند الفزع\" — the flesh between the side and shoulder blade that trembles at fright\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الفريصة: لحمة بين الجنب والكتف ترعد عند الفزع\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"ارتعدت فرائصه: من شدة الخوف\"\n- Root: عما (ʿAmā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"العمى: ذهاب البصر. والعمى: ذهاب البصيرة\" — blindness of sight; blindness of insight\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"العمى: آفة في البصر، وقد يستعار لآفة البصيرة\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"عمي القلب: ذهبت بصيرته\"\n- Root: أبصر (Abṣara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"البصر: حاسة الرؤية. والبصيرة: العلم\" — sight; insight\n  *Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الإبصار: إدراك البصر، وقد يستعار للبصيرة\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"أبصر الحق: علمه. ومنه: البصيرة\"\n- Root: كأس (Ka's)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الكأس: القدح إذا كان فيه شراب\" — the cup when it contains drink\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الكأس: الإناء المعروف، وقد يقال لما فيه\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"الكأس: القدح إذا كان فيه شراب\"", "book_title": "Audition and Ecstasy", "section_title_ar": "Section 511", "section_title_en": "Section 511", "section_index": 511, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Audition and Ecstasy'", "badge": "Afterlife"}], "87": [{"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذلك ذم الله تعالى قوما جعلوا لله ما يكرهون فقال تعال", "english": "The heart is not treated save with the compound electuary (maʿjūn) of knowledge and works. And this condition among the conditions of almsgiving runs in the same course as humility (al-khushūʿ) runs in the prayer. This is established by his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"A man has nothing of his prayer save that of which he was conscious.\" And this is like his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"Allah does not accept the charity of the one who bestows it with reproach (al-mannān).\" And like His saying — Exalted is He: {«لا تبطلوا صدقاتكم بالمن والأذى»} (\"Do not nullify your charities by reproach and injury\"). As for the legal opinion (fatwā) of the jurist that the alms fall in their proper place and that one's liability is discharged without this condition — that is another discussion, and we have already pointed to its meaning in the Book of Prayer.", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "وسلم فقال نعم إن الله لا يحب المستكبرين فنزل وقعد معهم على الأرض وأكل ثم سلم عليهم وركب وقال قد أجبتكم فأجيبوني قالوا نعم فوعدهم وقتا معلوما فحضروا فقدم إليهم فاخر الطعام وجلس يأكل معهم وأما قول القائل إن من وضعت يدي في قصعته فقد ذلت له رقبتي فقد قال بعضهم هذا خلاف السنة وليس كذلك فإنه ذل إذا كان الداعي لا يفرح بالإجابة ولا يتقلد منة وكان يرى ذلك يدا له على المدعو ورسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم كان يحضر لعلمه أن الداعي له يتقلد منة ويرى ذلك شرفا وذخرا لنفسه في الدنيا", "english": "The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Do not burden your guest so that you make him hate you, for whoever hates his guest has hated Allah, and whoever hates Allah, Allah hates him.\"", "book_title": "Condemnation of Anger and Hatred", "section_title_ar": "Section 290", "section_title_en": "The Etiquettes of Hospitality: Invitation, Response, Attendance, and the Serving of Food — Section 290 of The Revival of the Religious Sciences", "section_index": 290, "anchors": "- Root: ضيف (ḍ-y-f)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is \"to incline, to lean toward.\" The guest (al-ḍayf) is one who inclines toward you, alighting as a visitor; the root carries the sense of leaning or bending.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The guest is \"one who inclines to you, alighting with you\"; hospitality (al-ḍiyāfah) became customary in the sense of receiving such a one. The word is originally a verbal noun, hence one and many are equal in most usage.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"ḍāfa ilayhi: he inclined toward him\"; \"ḍāfat al-shams: the sun inclined toward setting.\" Metaphorical (majāz): \"aḍāfa ilayhi amran: he attributed a matter to him, leaning it upon him\"; \"al-muḍāf: the one constrained, hemmed in, in distress.\"\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and Focused Predication (al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd bi-innamā) — governing the Prophet's statement \"innamā hādhihi al-akhlāq bi-yad Allāh\" (these morals are only in the hand of Allah), where innamā restricts the attribution of moral character exclusively to divine bestowal.", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Condemnation of Anger and Hatred'", "badge": "Ethics"}], "89": [{"arabic": "الله تعالى {لا يؤاخذكم الله باللغو في أيمانكم} هذا ما نقل من الأقاويل ومن طلب الحق في التقليد فمهما استقصى تعارضت عنده هذه الأقاويل فيبقى متحيرا أومائلا إلى بعض الأقاويل بالتشهي وكل ذلك قصور بل ينبغي أن يطلب الحق بطريقه وذلك بالبحث عن مدارك الحظر والإباحة كما سنذكره بيان الدليل على إباحة السماع اعلم أن قول القائل السماع حرام معناه أن الله تعالى يعاقب عليه وهذا أمر لا يعرف بمجرد العقل بل بالسمع ومعرفة الشرعيات محصورة في النص أو القياس على المنصوص وأعني بالنص ما أظهره صلى الله عليه وسلم", "english": "And it was related from Ṭāhir ibn Bilāl al-Hamadānī al-Warrāq, who was among the people of knowledge, that he said: I was in spiritual retreat (muʿtakif) in the congregational mosque of Jeddah by the sea, and one day I saw a group reciting poetry (yaqūlūn qawl) in one corner of it and listening. I disapproved of that in my heart and said: In a house among the houses of God they recite poetry? He said: Then I saw the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, that night, seated in that very corner, and beside him was Abu Bakr al-Ṣiddīq, may God be pleased with him — and behold, Abu Bakr was reciting something of poetry, and the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, was listening to him and placing his hand upon his chest like one overcome with ecstasy (al-wājid) thereby. So I said to myself: It was not fitting for me to disapprove of those who were listening — for here is the Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, listening, and Abu Bakr reciting. Then he [the Prophet] turned to me and said: \"This is true by truth\" — or he said: \"True from truth\" — I am doubtful about it [the exact wording].", "book_title": "Audition and Ecstasy", "section_title_ar": "Section 488", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 488: On the Lawfulness of Auditory Samaʿ (Spiritual Listening)", "section_index": 488, "anchors": "- Root: سمع (s-m-ʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning: hearing, listening, perceiving by the ear; also used for answering/accepting a supplication.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Hearing as a physical sense perception; in theological usage, al-samʿ (revelation/textual report) is contrasted with al-ʿaql (reason) as a source of knowledge — precisely the dichotomy Ghazali invokes here.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): auditory perception. Metaphorical (majāz): obedience, acceptance, or the revealed text itself (al-samʿ = the transmitted revelation).\n- Root: طيب (ṭ-y-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning: goodness, pleasantness, purity, lawfulness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"That which the senses and the soul find pleasurable\"; in legal-theological terms, what is lawful (ḥalāl) and wholesome — both immediately and in its ultimate consequence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: pleasant to the senses. Metaphorical: lawful (ḥalāl), pure, free of taint — as in \"ṭāba lī kadhā\" (this became lawful/pleasant for me).\n- Root: قيس (q-y-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning: measuring, estimating, comparing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Qiyās in juristic theology: analogical extension of a ruling from an original case (al-aṣl) to a subsidiary case (al-farʿ) based on shared effective cause (ʿillah).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: measurement by measure or rod. Metaphorical: intellectual analogy and juridical reasoning.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — governing the periodic suspension of grammatical completion to allow for parenthetical or conditional insertions, as in Ghazali's layered conditional structures.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Audition and Ecstasy'", "badge": "Insight"}], "90": [{"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذلك ذم الله تعالى قوما جعلوا لله ما يكرهون فقال تعال", "english": "The heart is not treated save with the compound electuary (maʿjūn) of knowledge and works. And this condition among the conditions of almsgiving runs in the same course as humility (al-khushūʿ) runs in the prayer. This is established by his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"A man has nothing of his prayer save that of which he was conscious.\" And this is like his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"Allah does not accept the charity of the one who bestows it with reproach (al-mannān).\" And like His saying — Exalted is He: {«لا تبطلوا صدقاتكم بالمن والأذى»} (\"Do not nullify your charities by reproach and injury\"). As for the legal opinion (fatwā) of the jurist that the alms fall in their proper place and that one's liability is discharged without this condition — that is another discussion, and we have already pointed to its meaning in the Book of Prayer.", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Insight"}], "94": [{"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذلك ذم الله تعالى قوما جعلوا لله ما يكرهون فقال تعال", "english": "The heart is not treated save with the compound electuary (maʿjūn) of knowledge and works. And this condition among the conditions of almsgiving runs in the same course as humility (al-khushūʿ) runs in the prayer. This is established by his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"A man has nothing of his prayer save that of which he was conscious.\" And this is like his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"Allah does not accept the charity of the one who bestows it with reproach (al-mannān).\" And like His saying — Exalted is He: {«لا تبطلوا صدقاتكم بالمن والأذى»} (\"Do not nullify your charities by reproach and injury\"). As for the legal opinion (fatwā) of the jurist that the alms fall in their proper place and that one's liability is discharged without this condition — that is another discussion, and we have already pointed to its meaning in the Book of Prayer.", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Insight"}], "95": [{"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذلك ذم الله تعالى قوما جعلوا لله ما يكرهون فقال تعال", "english": "The heart is not treated save with the compound electuary (maʿjūn) of knowledge and works. And this condition among the conditions of almsgiving runs in the same course as humility (al-khushūʿ) runs in the prayer. This is established by his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"A man has nothing of his prayer save that of which he was conscious.\" And this is like his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"Allah does not accept the charity of the one who bestows it with reproach (al-mannān).\" And like His saying — Exalted is He: {«لا تبطلوا صدقاتكم بالمن والأذى»} (\"Do not nullify your charities by reproach and injury\"). As for the legal opinion (fatwā) of the jurist that the alms fall in their proper place and that one's liability is discharged without this condition — that is another discussion, and we have already pointed to its meaning in the Book of Prayer.", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Insight"}], "101": [{"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذلك ذم الله تعالى قوما جعلوا لله ما يكرهون فقال تعال", "english": "The heart is not treated save with the compound electuary (maʿjūn) of knowledge and works. And this condition among the conditions of almsgiving runs in the same course as humility (al-khushūʿ) runs in the prayer. This is established by his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"A man has nothing of his prayer save that of which he was conscious.\" And this is like his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"Allah does not accept the charity of the one who bestows it with reproach (al-mannān).\" And like His saying — Exalted is He: {«لا تبطلوا صدقاتكم بالمن والأذى»} (\"Do not nullify your charities by reproach and injury\"). As for the legal opinion (fatwā) of the jurist that the alms fall in their proper place and that one's liability is discharged without this condition — that is another discussion, and we have already pointed to its meaning in the Book of Prayer.", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Insight"}], "105": [{"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذلك ذم الله تعالى قوما جعلوا لله ما يكرهون فقال تعال", "english": "The heart is not treated save with the compound electuary (maʿjūn) of knowledge and works. And this condition among the conditions of almsgiving runs in the same course as humility (al-khushūʿ) runs in the prayer. This is established by his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"A man has nothing of his prayer save that of which he was conscious.\" And this is like his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"Allah does not accept the charity of the one who bestows it with reproach (al-mannān).\" And like His saying — Exalted is He: {«لا تبطلوا صدقاتكم بالمن والأذى»} (\"Do not nullify your charities by reproach and injury\"). As for the legal opinion (fatwā) of the jurist that the alms fall in their proper place and that one's liability is discharged without this condition — that is another discussion, and we have already pointed to its meaning in the Book of Prayer.", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "{يا أيها الذين آمنوا عليكم أنفسكم لا يضركم من ضل إذا اهتديتم} وإنكم تضعونها في غير موضعها وإني سمعت رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم يقول إذا رأى الناس المنكر فلم يغيروه أوشك أن يعمهم الله بعقاب (1) وقد قال صلى الله عليه وسلم إن الله ليسأل العبد حتى يقول له ما منعك إذا رأيت المنكر أن تنكره فإذا لقن الله لعبد حجته قال يا رب رجوتك وخفت الناس (2) وهذا إذا خاف من ضرب أو أمر لا يطاق ومعرفة حدود ذلك مشكلة وفيه خطر وفي العزلة خلاص وفي الأمر بالمعروف والنهي عن المنكر إثارة للخصومات وتحريك لغوائل الصدور كما قيل وكم سقت في", "english": "And for this reason one of the sages said: Man is only estranged from himself (innamā yastaʾnisu al-insān min nafsihi) because his essence is empty of virtue. So then he multiplies his meetings with people and drives away his loneliness by being with them. But when his essence is virtuous, he seeks solitude to aid himself in reflection (al-fikrah) and to extract knowledge and wisdom. And it has been said: \"Sociability (al-istīʾnās) is among the signs of bankruptcy.\" So this is a great benefit — but only for certain elite individuals (al-khawāṣṣ) and for one to whom is facilitated, through perpetual remembrance (dawām al-dhikr), intimacy with God (al-uns bi-Allāh), or through perpetual reflection (dawām al-fikr), verification in the knowledge of God. For such a one, total detachment is better than everything connected with social mixing (al-mukhālaṭah).", "book_title": "Enjoining Good and Forbidding Evil", "section_title_ar": "Section 449", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 449: On the Benefits of Seclusion (Fawāʾid al-ʿUzlah)", "section_index": 449, "anchors": "- Root: انس (ʾ-n-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of sociability, familiarity, and the dispelling of loneliness through company; the opposite of waḥshah (wilderness-like solitude).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-ʿuns is the heart's repose through the presence of what is beloved; its highest form is al-uns bi-Allāh — the soul's intimate familiarity with God achieved through perpetual remembrance (dhikr).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): human companionship. Metaphorical (majāz): \"He is the son of the intimacy of so-and-so\" — i.e., his special confidant; and the fire is called al-ʾanīsah because it dispels the night's loneliness.\n- Root: خلط (kh-l-ṭ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The mixing or blending of two or more things; a companion, neighbor, or partner is called khalīṭ.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-khalṭ is the union of parts of two or more things — broader than mazj (fusion). From it derives khalīṭān (two partners) in jurisprudence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: mixing water with wine. Metaphorical: \"I mixed with so-and-so\" — meaning intimate social intercourse; \"he is my khalīṭ in trade\" — i.e., my partner. The verse {«وَإِنَّ كَثِيرًا مِنَ الْخُلَطَاءِ لَيَبْغِي بَعْضُهُمْ عَلَى بَعْضٍ»} (Ṣād: 24) — \"And indeed many partners oppress one another.\"\n- Root: غيب (gh-y-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: That which is hidden from the eye; absence.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-ghayb is what is concealed from the senses and from human knowledge — but nothing is ghayb with respect to God, for nothing is hidden from Him.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"I met him at the setting of the sun\" (ghaybūbat al-shams). Metaphorical: \"They came in a ghābah\" — i.e., amidst dense spears like tangled trees.\n- Root: نكر (n-k-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Disapproval, rejection, or failure to recognize.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-inkār is the opposite of recognition (ʿirfān); the heart's rejection of what it does not conceive. It is also used for verbal disapproval — and the command to disapprove evil (al-nahy ʿan al-munkar) is a pillar of religion.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"I disapproved of the thing\" (ankartu al-shayʾ). Metaphorical: the tongue may deny what the heart affirms — thus hypocrisy enters.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Bāb al-ḥaṣr wa-al-taqyīd bi-innamā (Restriction and Focused Predication) — The particle innamā restricts the predicate to the subject alone, as in the sage's saying: \"إنما يستوحش الإنسان من نفسه\" — restricting the cause of spiritual loneliness to the soul's emptiness of virtue, not to the absence of companions.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Enjoining Good and Forbidding Evil'", "badge": "Insight"}], "106": [{"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذلك ذم الله تعالى قوما جعلوا لله ما يكرهون فقال تعال", "english": "The heart is not treated save with the compound electuary (maʿjūn) of knowledge and works. And this condition among the conditions of almsgiving runs in the same course as humility (al-khushūʿ) runs in the prayer. This is established by his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"A man has nothing of his prayer save that of which he was conscious.\" And this is like his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"Allah does not accept the charity of the one who bestows it with reproach (al-mannān).\" And like His saying — Exalted is He: {«لا تبطلوا صدقاتكم بالمن والأذى»} (\"Do not nullify your charities by reproach and injury\"). As for the legal opinion (fatwā) of the jurist that the alms fall in their proper place and that one's liability is discharged without this condition — that is another discussion, and we have already pointed to its meaning in the Book of Prayer.", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Insight"}], "109": [{"arabic": "عما كانوا يعملون} فيبدأ سبحانه بالأنبياء {يوم يجمع الله الرسل فيقول}", "english": "Then reflect, O wretched one, after these states upon what is directed against you of the questioning — face to face, without any interpreter — and you shall be asked concerning the little and the much, the speck on a date-stone (al-naqīr) and the husk (al-qitmīr). For while you are amidst the distress of the Resurrection, its sweat, and the severity of its terrors, lo! the angels descend from the horizons of the heaven with immense bodies and gigantic forms — harsh, severe — commanded to seize the forelocks of the criminals and drag them to the standing-place of presentation (mawqif al-ʿarḍ) before the Omnipotent (al-Jabbār). The Messenger of Allah, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, said: \"Verily, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has an angel between whose two eyelids is a distance of a hundred years' journey.\" So what is your assumption concerning yourself when you behold the likes of these angels — dispatched unto you to seize you and bring you to the station of presentation — and you see them, despite the enormity of their forms, broken in humility before the severity of that Day, filled with dread at what has become manifest of the Omnipotent's wrath upon His servants? And at their descent, there remains no prophet, no veracious one (ṣiddīq), no righteous soul, save that they fall upon their chins in fear, lest they themselves be the seized ones. Such is the state of those brought near (al-muqarrabūn) — so what is your assumption concerning the disobedient criminals?", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1340", "section_title_en": "The Description of the Day of Resurrection, Its Calamities, and Its Names — The Description of the Questioning", "section_index": 1340, "anchors": "- Root: س-أ-ل (sa-ʾa-la)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes seeking, demanding, or interrogating; al-suʾāl is the act of asking, and musāʾalah implies reciprocal or formal interrogation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological context, al-suʾāl on the Day of Resurrection is a divine inquest (musāʾalah) demanding account of deeds, not for God's knowledge (as He is 'alīm) but for establishing proof (ḥujjah) against the creature.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage is verbal inquiry; metaphorical (majāz) extends to the juridical reckoning (muḥāsabah) where the tongue is made to testify without intermediary.\n- Root: ن-ز-ل (na-za-la)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root signifies descent from a height; nuzūl is the act of alighting.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): When predicated of angels, it denotes their dispatch from the celestial realm to execute divine command — a descent of authority, not spatial movement in the divine essence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical descent; metaphorical: the advent of divine decree or angelic execution of wrath.\n- Root: ع-ر-ض (ʿa-ra-ḍa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root means to present, expose, or show; al-ʿarḍ is the exhibition of something before a viewer.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In eschatological usage, mawqif al-ʿarḍ is the standing-place of presentation before God for judgment — a public display of deeds.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to show; metaphorical: the juridical tribunal of the Hereafter where all acts are exhibited.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Interrogative Scope (Bāb al-istifhām) — The rhetorical question \"فما ظنك\" (so what is your assumption?) functions as a periodic pause, redirecting the addressee's cognition from narrated events to self-projection, governed by the conditional-temporal particle \"إذ\" (when) that binds the apodosis to the protasis.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Hadith"}, {"arabic": "{ماذا أجبتم قالوا لا علم لنا إنك أنت علام الغيوب} فيا لشدة يوم تذهل فيه عقول الأنبياء وتنمحى علومهم من شدة الهيبة إذ يقال لهم ما أجبتم وقد أرسلتم إلى الخلائق وكانوا قد علموا فتدهش عقولهم فلا يدرون بماذا يجيبون فيقولون من شدة الهيبة لا علم لنا إنك أنت علام الغيوب وهم فى ذلك الوقت صادقون إذ طارت منهم العقول وانمحت العلوم إلى أن يقويهم الله تعالى فيدعى نوح عليه السلام فيقال له هل بلغت فيقول نعم فيقال لأمته هل بلغكم فيقولون ما أتانا من نذير ويؤتى بعيسى عليه السلام فيقول الله تعالى له {أأنت قلت للنا", "english": "{«ماذا أجبتم قالوا لا علم لنا إنك أنت علام الغيوب»} — \"What did you answer?\" They will say: \"We have no knowledge; indeed, You alone are the Knower of the unseen.\" {Quran 5:109} So woe betide the severity of a Day in which the intellects of the prophets are stupefied and their knowledges are effaced by the sheer intensity of the awe, when it is said to them: \"What did you answer, when you were sent to the creatures?\" — though they had known — yet their intellects are bewildered, and they know not with what to reply. So they say, from the intensity of the awe: \"We have no knowledge; indeed, You alone are the Knower of the unseen.\" And they are, at that very moment, truthful, for the intellects have flown away from them and the knowledges have been effaced — until God Almighty strengthens them. Then Noah (peace be upon him) is summoned, and it is said to him: \"Did you convey the message?\" He says: \"Yes.\" Then it is said to his community: \"Did he convey to you?\" They say: \"No warner came to us.\"", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1341", "section_title_en": "The Day of Reckoning: The Questioning of the Prophets and the Exposure of the Servant Before the Lord of the Worlds", "section_index": 1341, "anchors": "- Root: رسل (R-S-L)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of \"sending forth with ease and gentleness\"; a messenger (rasūl) is one dispatched with a message.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الرسول\" denotes one sent with a commission; the root conveys both the act of dispatch and the message borne. In the eschatological context, the prophets (rusul) are asked \"ماذا أجبتم\" — what answer did they give to those to whom they were sent.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqīqah): \"أرسل الله في الأمم رسلا\" — God sent messengers among the nations. Metaphorical (majāz): \"أرسل الله عليهم العذاب\" — God loosed chastisement upon them. Here, the sending of prophets is literal, but the terror of the Day is such that even their knowledge is effaced.\n- Root: علم (ʿ-L-M)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Knowledge, perception, certainty; the opposite of ignorance.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — knowledge is the apprehension of a thing in its true reality. On the Day of Terror, the prophets' knowledge (ʿulūmuhum) is erased (tunmaḥū) by the sheer overwhelming majesty (haybah) of the Divine address, leaving them to answer: \"لا علم لنا\" — \"We have no knowledge.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"علمت الشيء أعلمه\" — I knew the thing. Here, the effacement of knowledge is a literal event of the Hour, not metaphorical, for the prophets are truthful when they disavow knowledge in that state.\n- Root: يوم (Y-W-M)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A day; a period of time, whether short or long.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"اليوم يعبر به عن وقت طلوع الشمس إلى غروبها. وقد يعبر به عن مدة من الزمان أي مدة كانت\" — the day is the time from sunrise to sunset, but it may denote any span of time. The \"Day\" of Resurrection is a duration whose terrors are magnified beyond earthly measure.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"ما رأيته اليوم\" — I did not see him today. Metaphorical: \"ذكر في أيام العرب كذا أي في وقائعها\" — \"days\" of the Arabs means their battles. The \"Day\" of Judgment is a real day, but its horrors are such that the prophets themselves are stupefied.\n- Root: الم (ʾ-L-M)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Severe pain, anguish.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الألم الوجع الشديد\" — pain is intense suffering. The \"عذاب أليم\" is a painful chastisement. In this passage, the terror of the Day is an \"أليم\" affliction upon the hearts of all creatures.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"مسه بضرب أليم\" — he touched him with a painful blow. The anguish of the Day is literal and inescapable.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The construction \"إنك أنت علام الغيوب\" employs the emphatic pronoun \"أنت\" as a focused predication, restricting the attribute of absolute knowledge of the unseen to God alone, even as the prophets disavow all knowledge before Him.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "110": [{"arabic": "خارج وهو المراد بقوله عز وجل {إذ أيدتك بروح القدس} وتقرب منه العصمة وهى", "english": "PageV04P107\nBut if one does not know what constitutes the welfare of his hereafter—so that he even supposes corruption to be welfare—then from where would mere volition benefit him? There is no benefit in volition, power, and causes except after guidance (hidāyah). For this reason, God—exalted is He—said: {«ربنا الذي أعطى كل شيء خلقه ثم هدى»} \"Our Lord who gave everything its creation, then guided.\" And He said—exalted is He—: {«ولولا فضل الله عليكم ورحمته ما زكى منكم أحد أبدا ولكن الله يزكي من يشاء»} \"And were it not for God's bounty upon you and His mercy, not one of you would ever be purified; but God purifies whom He wills.\" And the Prophet—peace be upon him—said: \"No one enters Paradise except by the mercy of God—exalted is He—,\" that is, by His guidance. So it was said: \"Not even you, O Messenger of God?\" He said: \"Not even I\" (1). And guidance (hidāyah) has three stations (manāzil):", "book_title": "Patience and Gratitude", "section_title_ar": "Section 999", "section_title_en": "The Degrees of Divine Guidance (Hidāyah), Righteous Discernment (Rushd), Straightening (Tasdīd), and Divine Support (Ta'yīd)", "section_index": 999, "anchors": "- Root: هدي (H-D-Y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of gentle, gracious direction (dalālah bi-luṭf); the root also yields \"gift\" (hadiyyah) and the leading beasts of prey (hawādī) that guide the herd.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Guidance is \"directional indication with gentleness\" (dalālah bi-luṭf), distinct from mere intellectual proof; it is a divine act that may be general (to all) or specific (to the elect), and its withholding is a form of abandonment.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"He guided him to the path\" (hadāhu lil-sabīl). Metaphorical (majāz): \"He guided him\" meaning \"he preceded him\" (taqaddamahu), as a leader precedes the caravan; hence guidance is not merely pointing but active leading.\n- Root: رشد (R-SH-D)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The state of being rightly directed in one's affairs; integrity of judgment; names Rāshid, Rushayd derive from it.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Rushd is the opposite of ghayy (error/ruin); it is used like hidāyah but with a nuance of practical, executive discernment—knowing the good AND being moved to pursue it. Al-Raghib explicitly distinguishes the rushd of Abraham (given before, as a special gift) from the mere legal competence (rushd) of an orphan.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"A man is rāshid or rashīd\" (possessing rightness in conduct). Metaphorical: \"He is of sound lineage\" (huwa li-rushdah), i.e., legitimate—showing the root's extension to ontological soundness.\n- Root: سدد (S-D-D)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To aim correctly, to hit the mark; the arrow that flies straight (sadīd); the opposite of deviation (ighrāḍ).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): (Not in the retrieved scholia, but by extension from the root's usage in the Qur'an, e.g., {wa-saddidū}, \"and be straight/aim rightly\")—it denotes the divine facilitation of the creature's movements so they align with the intended good.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"He aimed the arrow and it went straight.\" Metaphorical: \"He was granted success in speech and action\" (sudda li-l-ṣawāb).\n- Root: أيد (A-Y-D)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Strength, support, reinforcement; the \"aid\" (ayd) of God is His empowering of His servant.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): (By extension from {إذ أيدتك بروح القدس})—divine reinforcement through a holy spirit, i.e., an immaterial power that fortifies both inner perception (baṣīrah) and outer action.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"He strengthened him\" (ayyadahu). Metaphorical: Used for the conferral of spiritual power that makes the servant invincible against error.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Gradation and Specification (Tartīb wa-Takhṣīṣ) — The author structures a sequence of terms (hidāyah → rushd → tasdīd → ta'yīd) where each subsequent term is syntactically and semantically superordinate, building a hierarchy of divine acts. The particle \"wa-ammā\" (وأما) marks a new topic with contrastive focus, and the anaphoric references (هو، وهي) bind the exposition into a single periodic argument.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Patience and Gratitude'", "badge": "Insight"}], "114": [{"arabic": "حرمك واعف عمن ظلمك // ورأيت في الإنجيل قال عيسى بن مريم عليه السلام لقد قيل لكم من قبل إن السن بالسن والأنف بالأنف وأنا أقول لكم لا تقاوموا بالشر بل من ضرب خدك الأيمن فحول إليه الخد الأيسر ومن أخذ رداءك فأعطه إزارك ومن سخرك لتسير معه ميلا فسر معه ميلين وكل ذلك أمر بالصبر على الأذى فالصبر على أذى الناس من أعلى مراتب الصبر لأنه يتعاون فيه باعث الدين وباعث الشهوة والغضب جميعا", "english": "And acts of disobedience are the requisite of the impulse of caprice (bāʿith al-hawā). And the most severe kind of patience is patience in abstaining from those acts of disobedience that have become familiar through habit — for habit is a fifth nature. When habit is joined to lustful desire, two battalions from the battalions of Satan array themselves in concert against the battalion of God (jund Allāh) — may He be exalted — and the impulse of religion (bāʿith al-dīn) has no strength to suppress them. Then, if that act be one whose commission has been rendered easy (tayassara), patience in abstaining from it is heavier upon the soul — such as patience in abstaining from the sins of the tongue: backbiting (ghībah), falsehood, contentious disputation (mirāʾ), self-praise whether by insinuation (taʿrīḍ) or by explicit statement (taṣrīḥ), the varieties of jesting that wound hearts, the kinds of words by which one intends belittlement and contempt, and the mention of the dead with aspersion cast upon them, upon their sciences, their conduct, and their stations. For such mention is in its outward aspect (fī ẓāhirihi) backbiting, and in its inward aspect (fī bāṭinihi) self-praise. In it the soul has two lusts: one is the negation of the other [person], and the other is the affirmation of oneself. And by these two together there is completed for it that lordship (rubūbiyyah) which is innate in its disposition — and which is the very opposite of what it has been commanded regarding servanthood (ʿubūdiyyah). And because of the conjunction of the two lusts, and the ease of moving the tongue, and the fact that this becomes habitual in everyday converse, patience in abstaining from it becomes difficult. And it is among the greatest of the destructive enormities (al-mūbiqāt) — so much so that its disapproval and ugliness have been effaced from hearts by dint of its frequent repetition and the universality of familiarity with it. Thus you see a man who would, for example, wear silk and deem it utterly remote [of permissibility], yet he unleashes his tongue the whole day long against the reputations (aʿrāḍ) of people and does not deem that objectionable — notwithstanding what has come down in the report that backbiting is more severe than adultery (zinā). And God — may He be exalted — said: {«ودع أذاهم وتوكل على الله»} \"And leave aside their harm and put thy trust in God\" (Q. 33:48). And He said — may He be exalted: {«واصبر على ما يقولون واهجرهم هجرا جميلا»} \"And be patient over what they say, and depart from them with a gracious departure\" (Q. 73:10). And He said — may He be exalted: {«ولقد نعلم أنك يضيق صدرك بما يقولون فسبح بحمد ربك»} \"And indeed We know that thy breast is straitened by what they say; so glorify with the praise of thy Lord\" (Q. 15:97–98) — to the end of the verse. And He said — may He be exalted: {«ولتسمعن من الذين أوتوا الكتاب من قبلكم ومن الذين أشركوا أذى كثيرا وإن تصبروا وتتقوا فإن ذلك من عزم الأمور»} \"And you shall surely hear from those who were given the Scripture before you and from those who associate [others with God] much harm. And if you are patient and God-fearing, indeed that is of the resolute affairs\" (Q. 3:186) — that is, that you be patient in refraining from requital (mukāfaʾah). And for this reason God — may He be exalted — praised those who pardon others their due rights in retaliation (qiṣāṣ) and otherwise, saying — may He be exalted: {«وإن عاقبتم فعاقبوا بمثل ما عوقبتم به ولئن صبرتم لهو خير الصابرين»} \"And if you punish, then punish with the like of that wherewith you were afflicted. And if you are patient, it is surely better for the patient\" (Q. 16:126). And he — upon him be peace — said: \"Maintain the bond with him who cuts you off, and give to him who withholds from you, and pardon him who wrongs you.\" And I have seen in the Gospel that ʿĪsā ibn Maryam — upon him be peace — said: \"It was said to you of old: an eye for an eye and a nose for a nose. But I say to you: do not resist evil. Rather, whoever strikes your right cheek, turn to him PageV04P071 the left cheek; and whoever takes your cloak, give him your garment; and whoever compels you to walk with him one mile, walk with him two miles.\" And all of that is a command to be patient under harm. So patience under the harm of people is among the highest stations of patience, because in it the impulse of religion and the impulse of lustful desire and anger are all arrayed together [against the soul].", "book_title": "Patience and Gratitude", "section_title_ar": "Section 965", "section_title_en": "The Most Severe Forms of Patience: Abstaining from Habitual Sins and Enduring Harm", "section_index": 965, "anchors": "- Root: صبر (ṣ-b-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of restraint and withholding; \"ṣabara al-dābbah\" = to hold back an animal without fodder; \"ṣabartu nafsī\" = I restrained my soul.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الصبر: حبس النفس على ما يقتضيه العقل والشرع، أو عما يقتضيان حبسها عنه\" — Patience is the withholding of the soul upon what reason and Law require, or from what they require it to be withheld. When exercised against calamity it is called ṣabr alone (opposed to jazaʿ); in warfare, courage (shajāʿah); in vexing misfortune, forbearance.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"صبرت على ما أكره. وصبرت عما أحب\" — I bore patiently what I dislike, and I restrained myself from what I love. Metaphorical (majāz): \"هو أصبر على الضرب من الأرض\" — he is more enduring of beating than the earth.\n- Root: عادة (ʿ-w-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Recurrence and return; habitual practice that becomes second nature through repetition.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Habit (ʿādah) as reiterated action that imprints upon the soul, rendering the act facile and its abandonment burdensome.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: return to a state or place. Metaphorical: the ingrained disposition that governs conduct.\n- Root: غيب (gh-y-b)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الغيب: مصدر غابت الشمس وغيرها: إذا استترت عن العين\" — that which is concealed from the senses; applied to what is absent from human perception, though nothing is absent from God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"تكلم بذلك عن ظهر الغيب\" — he spoke of that behind the veil of the unseen. Metaphorical: \"أتونا في غابة\" — they came in a dense host (like tangled thickets).\n- Sibawayh Rule: Prepositional Interposition (باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور) — governing the permissible separation between the preposition and its object for rhetorical emphasis, as in the periodic construction \"فإن ذلك في ظاهره غيبة وفي باطنه ثناء على النفس\" where the circumstantial qualifiers (ẓāhir/bāṭin) are interposed to contrast outward form with inward reality.", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Patience and Gratitude'", "badge": "Ethics"}, {"arabic": "ينادي أين الزهاد في الدنيا زوروا عرس الزاهد في الدنيا عيسى ابن مريم وقال عيسى ابن مريم عليه السلام ويل لصاحب الدنيا كيف يموت ويتركها وما فيها وتغره ويأمنها ويثق بها وتخذله وويل للمغترين كيف أرتهم ما يكرهون وفارقهم ما يحبون وجاءهم ما يوعدون وويل لمن الدنيا همه والخطايا عمله كيف يفتضح غدا بذنبه وقيل أوحى الله تعالى إلى موسى عليه السلام يا موسى مالك ولدار الظالمين إنها ليست لك بدار أخرج منها همك وفارقها بعقلك فبئست الدار هي إلا لعامل يعمل فيها فنعمت الدار هي يا موسى إني مرصد للظالم حتى آخذ منه للمظلوم وروي أن رسول الله صلى الله عليه و", "english": "And lightning appeared one day, and he began seeking something to which he might take refuge. His eye fell upon a tent from afar, so he approached it — and behold, there was a woman within it. So he turned aside from her, and then there was a cave in a mountain. He came to it — and behold, there was a lion within it. So he placed his hand upon it and said: \"My God, Thou hast made for every thing a refuge, and Thou hast made for me no refuge.\" Then God — exalted be He — revealed to him: \"Thy refuge is in the resting place of My mercy. I shall surely wed thee on the Day of Resurrection to a hundred houris whom I created with My own hand, and I shall surely feed thee at thy wedding-feast for four thousand years — each day of which is like the age of this world — and I shall surely command a herald to proclaim: 'Where are the ascetics of the world? Come visit the wedding-feast of the ascetic of the world, Jesus son of Mary.'\"", "book_title": "Condemnation of the World", "section_title_ar": "Section 732", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Book on the Remembrance of Death: On the Merit of Renouncing the World (Section 732)", "section_index": 732, "anchors": "- Root: برق (b-r-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of shining, flashing, and appearing suddenly — from which \"al-barq\" (lightning) derives; also used for that which startles and alarms.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Used for sudden manifestation; here, the lightning serves as a natural sign prompting reflection on transience.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqiqah) for lightning; metaphorical (majaz) for the fleeting illumination of worldly life that vanishes instantly.\n- Root: زهد (z-h-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To abstain from, to disdain, to regard as little; al-zuhd is the renunciation of something while it remains within one's power.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The theological virtue of renouncing the world (al-zuhd fi al-dunya) — not the mere abandonment of wealth, but the emptying of the heart from attachment to it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to turn away from a thing with aversion; metaphorical: the soul's disengagement from worldly allurements while the body remains present.\n- Root: عرس (ʿ-r-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Wedding feast; the celebration of union; also used for the bridal chamber.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In the eschatological context, the \"wedding\" of the ascetic (al-zāhid) is a divine metaphor for the honor bestowed upon the renunciant in the Hereafter.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a human marriage celebration; metaphorical: the spiritual recompense of intimacy with the Divine.\n- Root: سخط (s-kh-t)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Displeasure, wrath, indignation; the opposite of ridā (satisfaction).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Divine displeasure (sakhat) as the cause of destruction; death by \"wrath\" (sakhtah) signifies a punishment visited upon a people for their disobedience.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: anger; metaphorical: the withdrawal of divine mercy and the sending of chastisement.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The permissibility of separating a prepositional phrase from its governed noun when the intervening element is semantically complete, as in the narrative transitions between reported speech and action.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Condemnation of the World'", "badge": "Afterlife"}], "116": [{"arabic": "خوفه لا محالة ولذلك قال المسيح صلى الله عليه وسلم لما قيل له أأنت قلت للناس اتخذوني وأمي إلهين من دون الله قال سبحانك ما يكون لي أن أقول ما ليس لي بحق إن كنت قلته فقد علمته تعلم ما في نفسي ولا أعلم ما في نفسك {وقال} إن تعذبهم فإنهم عبادك وإن تغفر لهم الآية فوض الأمر إلى المشيئة وأخرج نفسه بالكلية من البين لعلمه بأنه ليس له من الأمر شيء وأن الأمور مرتبطة بالمشيئة ارتباطا يخرج عن حد المعقولات والمألوفات فلا يمكن الحكم عليها بقياس ولا حدس ولا حسبان فضلا عن التحقيق والاستيقان وهذا هو الذي قطع قلوب العارفين إذ الطامة الكبرى هي ارتباط أمرك بمشيئة من لا يبالي", "english": "Just as Abraham (peace be upon him), when he was placed in the catapult, said: \"God is sufficient for me\" {حسبي الله} — and this was among the greatest supplications — he was tested and was confronted by Gabriel in mid-air, who said to him: \"Do you have any need?\" He replied: \"As for you, no.\" And that was the fulfillment of the very reality of his words: \"God is sufficient for me.\" So God Most High informed us about him, saying: \"And Abraham who fulfilled\" {وإبراهيم الذي وفى} — that is, fulfilled the requirement of his saying \"God is sufficient for me.\" And in like manner, He informed us about Moses (peace be upon him), where he said: \"Indeed, we fear that he may assault us or that he may transgress\" {إننا نخاف أن يفرط علينا أو أن يطغى}. He said: \"Fear not; indeed, I am with you both; I hear and I see\" {لا تخافا إنني معكما أسمع وأرى}. Yet despite this, when the sorcerers cast their sorcery, Moses conceived within himself a fear — because he did not feel secure from the deception (makar) of God, and the matter became obscure to him — until security was renewed for him, and it was said to him: \"Fear not; indeed, you are the most exalted\" {لا تخف إنك أنت الأعلى}. And when the strength of the Muslims weakened on the Day of Badr, he (peace be upon him) said: \"O God, if You destroy this band, there will remain no one on the face of the earth who worships You\" — a hadith reported on the Day of Badr: \"O God, if You destroy this band, there will remain no one on the face of the earth who worships You,\" transmitted by al-Bukhari from the hadith of Ibn Abbas with the wording: \"O God, if You will, You will not be worshipped after this day\" — to the end of the hadith. So Abu Bakr (may God be pleased with him) said: \"Leave off your importuning of your Lord, for He will surely fulfill what He has promised you.\" Thus, the station of al-Siddiq (may God be pleased with him) was the station of trust in God's promise, while the station of the Messenger of God (peace be upon him) was the station of fear of God's deception (makar) — and this latter is more complete, for it issues only from the perfection of knowledge of God's secrets, the hidden aspects of His acts, and the meanings of His attributes, some of whose manifestations are expressed as \"deception\" (makar). And no human being can stand upon the ultimate reality (kunnah) of God's attributes. Whoever knows the true nature of knowledge — that his knowledge falls short of encompassing the ultimate reality of things — his fear inevitably magnifies. For this reason, the Messiah (peace be upon him), when it was said to him: \"Did you say to the people: 'Take me and my mother as two gods besides God?'\" replied: \"Glory be to You! It was not mine to say what I had no right to say. If I had said it, You would have known it. You know what is in my self, and I do not know what is in Your Self\" {سبحانك ما يكون لي أن أقول ما ليس لي بحق إن كنت قلته فقد علمته تعلم ما في نفسي ولا أعلم ما في نفسك}. And he said: \"If You punish them, indeed they are Your servants; and if You forgive them...\" {إن تعذبهم فإنهم عبادك وإن تغفر لهم} — to the end of the verse. So he delegated the matter entirely to the divine will (mashī'ah) and removed himself completely from the equation, knowing that he had no say in the affair whatsoever, and that all matters are linked to the divine will in a linkage that exceeds the bounds of rational categories and habitual patterns — such that no judgment can be passed upon them by analogy, nor by conjecture, nor by reckoning (ḥisbān), let alone by verification and certainty. And this is precisely what has rent the hearts of the knowers (ʿārifīn) asunder — for the supreme calamity (al-ṭāmmah al-kubrā) is the linkage of your affair to the will of One who does not care about you: if He destroys you, He has destroyed countless others like you, and He has never ceased in this world to torment them with various kinds of pains and diseases, and to sicken their hearts thereby with disbelief and hypocrisy, and then to perpetuate the punishment upon them for all eternity. Then He informs us about this, saying: \"And had We willed, We would have given every soul its guidance, but the word from Me has taken effect: I will surely fill Hell with jinn and people all together\" {ولو شئنا لآتينا كل نفس هداها ولكن حق القول مني لأملأن جهنم من الجنة والناس أجمعين}. And He Most High said: \"And the word of your Lord has been fulfilled: I will surely fill Hell...\" {وتمت كلمة ربك لأملأن جهنم}. So how should one not fear what has been decreed as a word from eternity (al-azal), and not hope for its reversal? Had the matter been recent, then hopes might extend toward some stratagem regarding it — but there is nothing except submission to it, and the discernment of the hidden precedent (al-sābiqah) from the manifest outward causes upon the heart and the bodily members. For whoever is facilitated toward the causes of evil, and is barred from the causes of good, and whose attachment to this world is firmly secured — it is as if the secret of the prior decree that destined him to wretchedness has been unveiled to him with certainty, since \"everyone is facilitated toward that for which he was created\" {كل ميسر لما خلق له}. And even if all good things were facilitated, and the heart were entirely severed from this world, with its outward and inward aspects turned wholly toward God, this would necessitate a lightening of fear — were continuity in that state to be trusted. But the peril of the final end (khātimah) and the difficulty of steadfastness only fan the flames of fear into greater blaze and prevent them from being extinguished. For how can one feel secure from the change of state, when the believer's heart lies between two fingers of the fingers of the Merciful? And indeed, the heart is more prone to turning than a boiling pot. And the Turner of Hearts (Glory be to Him) has said: \"Indeed, the punishment of their Lord is not to be felt secure from\" {إن عذاب ربهم غير مأمون}. So the most ignorant of people is the one who feels secure from it, while He proclaims the warning against security. And were God to show kindness to His knowing servants by reviving their hearts with the spirit of hope (rajā'), their hearts would still burn from the fire of fear. Thus, the causes of hope are a mercy for God's elect, and the causes of heedlessness are a mercy upon the common folk of creation in one respect — for were the veil to be lifted, souls would perish and hearts would be torn asunder from the fear of the Turner of Hearts. One of the knowers said: \"If fifty were to intervene between me and the One I have known through tawḥīd...\"", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1051", "section_title_en": "The Reality of Fear and the Station of Abraham, Moses, and the Messenger (Peace Be Upon Them) — On the Inescapable Terror of the Divine Decree", "section_index": 1051, "anchors": "- Root: حسب (ḥ-s-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of counting, reckoning, and sufficiency; \"ḥasbī Allāh\" signifies God as the sufficient reckoner.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance of ḥisāb as divine accounting; \"ḥasbī Allāh\" is a declaration of total reliance on God's sufficiency, transcending mere numerical count.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) — reckoning by number; Metaphorical (majāz) — trusting in God's sufficiency as an act of spiritual accounting, where the servant counts God as enough against all else.\n- Root: خوف (kh-w-f)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of fear, dread, and apprehension of harm.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Khawf is the expectation of a disliked outcome based on a probable or known sign; it opposes amn (security) and applies to both worldly and otherworldly matters.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — fear of loss or harm; Metaphorical — a \"fearful path\" (ṭarīq khā'if) where danger inheres in the object, and \"takhawwuf\" as gradual diminishment, implying fear's erosive effect on the soul.\n- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of self, soul, breath; the essence of a thing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Nafs as the self or essence (dhāt), as in \"ta'lamu mā fī nafsī\" (You know what is in my self), denoting the innermost reality distinct from the body.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — the soul as animate essence; Metaphorical — \"nafs sā'ilah\" (flowing blood) as the life-principle, and the soul's hidden movements as the locus of divine knowledge.\n- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of turning, overturning, and transformation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Qalb is the turning of a thing from one state to another; the heart is so named for its constant fluctuation (taqallub) between states.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — physical overturning; Metaphorical — the heart's perpetual oscillation, as in the prophetic hadith of the heart between two fingers of the Merciful, and the \"turning\" of affairs by divine decree.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Interposition and Apposition (al-ʿaṭf wa al-faṣl) — The periodic structure of \"fa-kayfa lā yakhāfu...\" (How then should one not fear...) employs a rhetorical question governed by an implied causal clause, where the apodosis is suspended across multiple subordinate clauses, requiring the translator to preserve the cumulative force of the argument without breaking the syntactic chain.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "تعلم سري وعلانيتي فاقبل معذرتي وتعلم حاجتي فأعطني سؤلي وتعلم ما في نفسي فاغفر لي ذنوبي اللهم إني أسألك إيمانا يباشر قلبي ويقينا صادقا حتى أعلم أنه لن يصيبني إلا ما كتبته علي والرضا بما قسمته لي يا ذا الجلال والإكرام فأوحى الله عز وجل إليه إني قد غفرت لك ولم يأتني أحد من ذريتك فيدعوني بمثل الذي دعوتني به إلا غفرت له وكشفت غمومه وهمومه ونزعت الفقر من بين عينيه واتجرت له من وراء كل تاجر وجاءته الدنيا وهي راغمة وإن كان لا يريدها دعاء علي بن أبي طالب رضي الله عنه رواه عن النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم أنه ق", "english": "Muḥammad ibn Ḥassān said: Maʿrūf al-Karkhī — may God have mercy on him — said to me: \"Shall I not teach you ten words — five for this world and five for the Hereafter? Whoever supplicates God — Mighty and Majestic — with them shall find God — Most High — present with them.\" I said: \"Write them down for me.\" He said: \"No, but I shall repeat them to you as Bakr ibn Khunays — may God have mercy on him — repeated them to me:", "book_title": "On Invocations and Supplications", "section_title_ar": "Section 249", "section_title_en": "Section 249 — Supplications of the Righteous (Adʿiyat al-Ṣāliḥīn)", "section_index": 249, "anchors": "- Root: حسب (ḥ-s-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of counting, reckoning, and sufficiency — \"ḥasb\" denotes that which suffices or is enough\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحساب: استعمال العدد\" — the theological nuance of ḥasbī Allāh (\"God is my Sufficer\") carries the sense of complete reliance upon God's reckoning and provision, transcending mere numerical counting to signify total trust in divine sufficiency\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"حسب المال\" (he counted the wealth); Metaphorical (majāz): \"خرجا يتحسبان الأخبار\" (they go out seeking news) — in the supplication \"ḥasbī Allāh,\" the literal sense of \"sufficiency\" is intended, yet it carries the metaphorical extension of complete trust, as one who has God as his Sufficer has no need of any other reckoning\n\n- Root: اله (ʾ-l-h)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of worship, devotion, and seeking refuge; \"ilāh\" denotes that which is worshipped\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الله: قيل: أصله إله فحذفت همزته، وأدخل عليها الألف واللام، فخص بالباري تعالى\" — the name Allāh is the proper name of the Creator, distinguished from generic \"ilāh\" (any object of worship); in these supplications, the divine names invoked (al-Karīm, al-Ḥalīm, al-Qawī, al-Shadīd, al-Raḥīm, al-Raʾūf, al-Laṭīf, al-Qadīr) each specify particular attributes of the divine essence\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"فلان يتأله: يتعبد\" (he worships); the supplications employ the divine names in their literal theological sense, each name invoking a specific attribute relevant to the particular need mentioned\n\n- Root: انا (ʾ-n-y)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"أنا ضمير المخبر عن نفسه\" — the first-person pronoun; in the divine self-declaration \"innī anā Allāh,\" the repetition emphasizes divine self-identification\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"أنى الطعام أي إدراكه\" (the food ripened/reached its time) — the related sense of something reaching its appointed time appears in \"عند المسألة في القبر\" (at the questioning in the grave), indicating appointed moments of divine reckoning\n\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The supplications employ repeated prepositional phrases (ʿinda al-mawt, ʿinda al-masʾalah, ʿinda al-ḥisāb, ʿinda al-mīzān, ʿinda al-ṣirāṭ) in parallel construction, each governing a distinct divine name, creating a rhythmic liturgical structure where each clause stands as an independent invocation\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'On Invocations and Supplications'", "badge": "Insight"}], "117": [{"arabic": "وأنت المؤخر وأنت على كل شيء قدير (1) وقال علي رضي الله عنه كنت رجلا إذا سمعت من رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم حديثا نفعني الله عز وجل بما شاء أن ينفعني منه وإذا حدثني أحد من أصحابه استحلفته فإذا حلف صدقته قال وحدثني أبو بكر وصدق أبو بكر رضي الله عنه قال سمعت رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم يقول ما من عبد يذنب ذنبا فيحسن الطهور ثم يقوم فيصلي ركعتين ثم يستغفر الله عز وجل إلا غفر له ثم تلا قوله عز وجل والذين إذا فعلوا فاحشة أو ظلموا أنفسهم (2) الآية وروى أبو هريرة عن النبي صلى", "english": "God Most High said: {«والذين إذا فعلوا فاحشة أو ظلموا أنفسهم ذكروا الله فاستغفروا لذنوبهم»} — \"And those who, when they commit an indecency or wrong themselves, remember God and seek forgiveness for their sins\" {آل عمران/135}.", "book_title": "On Invocations and Supplications", "section_title_ar": "Section 246", "section_title_en": "On Seeking Forgiveness (Al-Istighfar) — Section 246", "section_index": 246, "anchors": "- Root: ذنب (dhanb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of \"tail\" or \"taking hold of a tail\"; extended to signify a sin as that which trails behind and follows a person.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الذنب في الأصل: الأخذ بذنب الشيء\" — sin is that by which one is seized and held accountable; used metaphorically for a fault that clings to its perpetrator.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: tail of an animal. Metaphorical: \"هو من الأذناب\" — one who follows; sin as that which trails the soul and must be severed through repentance.\n- Root: غفر (ghafara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Covering, veiling, protecting from defilement; a shield or helmet (المغفر).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الغفران والمغفرة من الله هو أن يصون العبد من أن يمسه العذاب\" — divine forgiveness is God's protection of the servant from being touched by punishment.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to cover and protect. Metaphorical: God's \"covering\" of sins — not merely erasure, but active safeguarding of the sinner from consequence.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The frequent interposition of \"يا رب\" and vocative constructions between the subject and predicate in supplicatory sentences, as in \"اللهم اغفر لي,\" where the vocative particle governs the imperative verb's addressee, reflecting the grammatical permissibility of separation between governing and governed elements in invocational speech.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'On Invocations and Supplications'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "118": [{"arabic": "يرددها وهي إن تعذبهم فإنهم عبادك وإن تغفر لهم (2) الآية وقام تميم الداري ليلة بهذه الآية {أم حسب الذين اجترحوا السيئات} الآية وقام سعيد بن جبير ليلة يردد هذه الآية وامتازوا اليوم أيها المجرمون وقال بعضهم إني لأفتتح السورة فيوقفني بعض ما أشهد فيها عن الفراغ منها حتى يطلع الفجر وكان بعضهم يقول آية لا أتفهمها ولا يكون قلبي فيها لا أعد لها ثوابا وحكي عن أبي سليمان الداراني أنه قال إني لأتلو الآية فأقيم فيها أربع ليال أو خمس ليال ولولا أني أقطع الفكر فيها ما جاوزتها إلى غيرها وعن", "english": "Ali, may God be pleased with him, said: \"There is no good in worship that lacks understanding (fiqh), nor in recitation that lacks reflection.\"", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 227", "section_title_en": "The Third Book on the Inward Acts During Recitation — Ten in Number", "section_index": 227, "anchors": "- Root: دبر (d-b-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The rear, the back part; what comes after; to turn one's back; the end or sequel of a thing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): That which is opposite of \"front\" (qabal); used metaphorically for what follows or comes after; \"adbar al-nahar\" — the latter part of the day; \"adbar al-sujud\" — the concluding portions of prayers.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the back or hind part. Metaphorical: what comes at the end, the sequel, the consequence of an affair.\n- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To turn over, to overturn, to transform; the heart as that which is constantly turning and fluctuating between states.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The turning and directing of a thing from one face to another; al-taqallub — the constant changing and fluctuation; the heart (qalb) is so named for its perpetual turning about of resolutions and states.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to turn a thing over from its face. Metaphorical: to ponder, to examine thoroughly, to turn a matter over in the mind — \"qallaba al-amr\" — he examined the affair from all sides.\n- Root: ختم (kh-t-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To seal, to stamp, to imprint; the seal or stamp itself.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Sealing and stamping — used both for the act of impressing and the impression left; metaphorically employed for securing a thing and preventing access to it, as in {«ختم الله على قلوبهم»} (God sealed their hearts).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: placing a seal upon something. Metaphorical: to conclude, to finish, to bring to an end — \"khatama al-Qur'an\" — he completed the recitation of the Quran.\n- Root: بقي (b-q-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To remain, to endure, to persist; the lasting and continuing of a thing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The persistence of a thing upon its original state; that which remains (al-baqi) is of two kinds: that which remains by itself without limit — and that is the Creator, may He be exalted — and that which remains by another, which is everything other than Him.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to remain, to stay. Metaphorical: \"baqiya al-shay'\" — the enduring of a thing; \"ma baqiyat minhum baqiyah\" — not one of them remained.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — When the construction \"innama\" is employed, it restricts the predicate to the subject alone, excluding all else. This is pertinent here as the author delimits the inward acts of recitation to precisely ten, no more and no less.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Insight"}], "119": [{"arabic": "وقال عز وجل {رضي الله عنهم ورضوا عنه ذلك لمن خشي ربه} وكل ما دل على فضيلة العلم دل على فضيلة الخوف لأن الخوف ثمرة العلم ولذلك جاء في خبر موسى عليه أفضل الصلاة والسلام وأما الخائفون", "english": "As for the path of derivation (iqtibās) from the verses and reports, what has been transmitted concerning the virtue of fear is beyond enumeration. And sufficient as an indication of its virtue is that God—may He be exalted—has gathered together for the fearful (khāʾifūn) guidance (hudā), mercy (raḥmah), knowledge (ʿilm), and good pleasure (riḍwān)—and these are the sum total of the stations of the people of Paradise. God—may He be exalted—said: {وهدى ورحمة للذين هم لربهم يرهبون} \"And guidance and mercy for those who of their Lord are fearful\" [Al-Aʿrāf: 154]. And He—exalted is He—said: {إنما يخشى الله من عباده العلماء} \"Only those of His servants who possess knowledge fear God\" [Fāṭir: 28]—describing them with knowledge on account of their fear [of Him].", "book_title": "Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment", "section_title_ar": "Section 1041", "section_title_en": "Exposition of the Virtue of Fear (Khawf) and Encouragement Thereof", "section_index": 1041, "anchors": "- Root: خوف (Khawf)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes trepidation, apprehension, and the state of being overtaken by dread of a feared outcome.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الخوف: توقع مكروه عن أمارة مظنونة، أو معلومة\" — the anticipation of something detestable based on a probable or certain indication; it is the direct opposite of amn (security) and applies to both worldly and otherworldly matters.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"خفته على مالي خوفا وخيفة\" — fearing for something. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"تخوفه: تنقصه\" — to diminish gradually, as fear consumes the soul piecemeal.\n- Root: فضل (Fadl)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Excess, superiority, or merit beyond the baseline of sufficiency.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الفضل: الزيادة عن الاقتصاد\" — increase beyond moderation; the praiseworthy type is like the surplus of knowledge and forbearance, and it is measured by its end (ghāyah).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"مال فلان فاضل: كثير يفضل عن القوت\" — surplus wealth exceeding necessity. The virtue of a thing is gauged by its instrumental efficacy toward a noble goal.\n- Root: علم (ʿIlm)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Certain cognition and apprehension of a thing as it truly is.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — apprehension of a thing's reality; it is the root from which khawf branches as its fruit.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"ما علمت بخبرك: ما شعرت به\" — to have cognizance. The scholars (ʿulamāʾ) are so named for their knowledge, which necessarily engenders fear of God.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and Focused Predication (باب الحصر والتقييد) — governing the exclusive attribution in {إنما يخشى الله من عباده العلماء}, where the predicate restricts fear to the knowledgeable, and the syntactic fronting of \"whom they fear\" (لربهم يرهبون) emphasizes the object of reverential dread.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment'", "badge": "Afterlife"}, {"arabic": "آلاف درهم لا أفعل ذلك أبدا رضي الله عنه", "english": "The Messiah, peace be upon him, said: \"With difficulty shall the rich man enter Paradise.\"", "book_title": "Poverty and Abstinence", "section_title_ar": "Section 1071", "section_title_en": "The Virtue of Poverty and the Condemnation of Wealth — Section 1071", "section_index": 1071, "anchors": "- Root: دخل (dakhala)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of entering, penetrating, or intruding into a space or state.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Often used for entering into a condition or abode, including the afterlife states of Paradise and Hell.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for physical entry; metaphorical for entering into a spiritual state or reckoning.\n- Root: فقر (faqara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To hollow out, pierce, or be in a state of neediness; the poor one is \"hollowed\" of worldly goods.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Denotes the state of one lacking sufficient means; in the Kalam context, it signifies a spiritual station of utter dependence upon God, not merely material lack.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for material destitution; metaphorical for the soul's essential neediness before its Lord.\n- Root: غني (ghaniya)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be rich, self-sufficient, or free from need.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological terms, absolute Ghina (Self-Sufficiency) belongs to God alone; human wealth is a relative accident (ʿaraḍ) that often breeds heedlessness.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for possessing abundance; metaphorical for the false sense of independence that veils one from God.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Prepositional Interposition (باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور) — Governing the syntactic flow of conditional and circumstantial clauses, ensuring the periodic structure of the narrative remains unbroken across multiple embedded quotations.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Poverty and Abstinence'", "badge": "Afterlife"}], "120": [{"arabic": "وهو مع ذلك قريب من كل موجود وهو أقرب إلى العبد من حبل الوريد وهو على كل شيء شهيد إذ لا يماثل قربه قرب الأجسام كما لا تماثل ذاته ذات الأجسام وأنه لا يحل في شيء ولا يحل فيه شيء تعالى عن أن يحويه مكان كما تقدس عن أن يحده زمان بل كان قبل أن خلق الزمان والمكان وهو الآن على ما عليه كان وأنه بائن عن خلقه بصفاته ليس في ذاته سواه ولا في سواه ذاته وأنه مقدس عن التغير والانتقال لا تحله الحوادث ولا تعتريه العوارض بل لا يزال في نعوت جلاله منزها عن الزوال وفي صفات كماله مستغنيا عن زيادة الاستكمال وأنه في ذاته معلوم الوجود بالعقول مرئ", "english": "- **Root: خلق (khalaqa)**\n  - *Lisan / Ayn:* Core meaning of measuring, proportioning, and fashioning; al-Khalil notes the root's application to the act of creation as bringing forth upon a measured determination.\n  - *Al-Raghib (Mufradat):* \"الخلق أصله: التقدير المستقيم\" — Creation fundamentally signifies right/straight measurement; used for bringing into being from nothing (ibdāʿ) as in {خلق السماوات والأرض}, and for bringing forth from something as in {خلقكم من نفس واحدة}.\n  - *Al-Zamakhshari (Asas):* Literal (ḥaqīqah): the craftsman's measuring before cutting. Metaphorical (majāz): \"خلق الله الخلق: أوجده على تقدير أوجبته الحكمة\" — God's creation is bringing into existence upon a measure necessitated by wisdom.", "book_title": "On Invocations and Supplications", "section_title_ar": "Section 72", "section_title_en": "Section 72", "section_index": 72, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'On Invocations and Supplications'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "الأصل الأول العلم بأن صانع العالم قادر وأنه تعالى في قوله وهو على كل شيء قدير صادق لأن العالم محكم في صنعته مرتب في خلقته ومن رأى ثوبا من ديباج حسن النسج والتأليف متناسب التطريز والتطريف ثم توهم صدور نسجه عن ميت لا استطاعة له أو عن إنسان لا قدرة له كان منخلعا عن غريزة العقل ومنخرطا في سلك أهل الغباوة والجهل الأصل الثاني العلم بأنه تعالى عالم بجميع الموجودات ومحيط بكل المخلوقات لا يعزب عن علمه مثقال ذرة في الأرض ولا في السماء صادق في قوله وهو بكل شيء عليم ومرشد إلى صدقه بقوله تعالى ألا يعلم من خلق وهو اللطيف الخبير أرشدك", "english": "**The Eighth Principle: Knowledge that He, Exalted is He, is established upon His Throne (mustawin ʿalā ʿarshihi) in the sense that God, Exalted is He, intended by istiwāʾ — which is the sense that does not contradict the attribute of Majesty, nor does any mark of origination (ḥudūth) or passing away (fanāʾ) attach to it. And it is the sense intended by His saying — istiwāʾ toward the heaven — where He said in the Qur'an: {«ثُمَّ اسْتَوَىٰ إِلَى السَّمَاءِ وَهِيَ دُخَانٌ»} (\"Then He directed Himself to the heaven, and it was smoke\") [Fussilat 41:11]. And that is only by way of subjugation (qahr) and dominion (istīlāʾ), as the poet said:", "book_title": "On Invocations and Supplications", "section_title_ar": "Section 90", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 90: The Ten Principles Concerning Knowledge of the Divine Attributes", "section_index": 90, "anchors": "- Root: ع ل م (ʿ-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denoting the faculty of perception and comprehension; the mark or trace by which something is known.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — knowledge is the apprehension of a thing in its true reality, divided into apprehension of the essence and judgment upon it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Distinguishes literal knowledge (ḥaqīqah) from awareness (shiʿr); here, it denotes certain, verified cognition of theological verities.\n- Root: س و ي (s-w-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Root indicating straightness, equality, and levelness; from it derives istiwāʾ (establishing oneself upon, ascending to).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Istiwāʾ upon the Throne (al-ʿArsh) is a divine act of sovereign dominion, not corporeal settlement.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal sense of physical sitting is impossible for God; metaphorical sense (majāz) of qahr (overpowering) and istīlāʾ (dominion) is necessitated by reason and consensus.\n- Root: ر أ ي (r-ʾ-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Visual perception; also opinion and discernment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ruʾyah (vision) of God in the Hereafter is a mode of disclosure (kashf) and knowledge (ʿilm), more complete than intellectual cognition, not conditioned by spatial direction.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal vision requires corporeality and direction; its application to God demands theological specification (takhṣīṣ) without nullifying the veracity of the scriptural promise.\n- Root: ق د ر (q-d-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Power, measure, decree; the capacity to bring about an act.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Divine power (qudrah) is the negation of incapacity (ʿajz); absolute power belongs to God alone, while creatures possess limited capacity.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal power over an object; metaphorically, God's power encompasses all things without exertion or instrument.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Prepositional Interposition (باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور) — governing the permissible separation between the preposition and its object for rhetorical emphasis, applied here to the parsing of \"إلى السماء\" in the verse on istiwāʾ, where the prepositional phrase is delayed to highlight the divine act of sovereign direction toward the heavens.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'On Invocations and Supplications'", "badge": "Hadith"}]}, "wordTiming": {"minshawi": {"1": [[1, 1240, 4625], [2, 4625, 6004], [3, 6004, 9222], [4, 9222, 11082], [5, 11082, 12795], [6, 12795, 13850], [7, 13850, 14477], [8, 14477, 15752], [9, 15752, 17173], [10, 17173, 18354], [11, 18354, 19127], [12, 19127, 20648], [13, 20648, 21870], [14, 21870, 22654], [15, 22654, 23793], [16, 23793, 25075], [17, 25075, 26454], [18, 26454, 2710